var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"Overview","text":"The public API of ModflowGPU, one page per area:\n\nRunning a simulation: run_simulation and its result, loading MF6 input, errors.\nBackends and precision: choosing the CPU or a GPU, and the precision presets.\nStepping API: running a simulation one time step at a time and reading or setting its variables, for coupling.\nBuilding models: a simulation from Julia arrays, written as MF6 input.\nOutput files: reading head, budget, binary grid and listing files.\nFor backend authors: the hooks a GPU package extension implements.\n\nNames written as ModflowGPU.name on these pages are public but not exported: write them qualified. All others are exported by using ModflowGPU.\n\nModflowGPU.run and ModflowGPU.finalize, the names used before version 0.1 exported anything, are the same functions as run_simulation and finalize_simulation and keep working. They are not exported because they would clash with Base.run and Base.finalize.\n\nThe BMI functions are methods of BasicModelInterface's own functions for Simulation and are called qualified, for example BasicModelInterface.update(sim); see BMI.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#ModflowGPU.ModflowGPU","page":"Overview","text":"ModflowGPU\n\nThe groundwater-flow (GWF) model of MODFLOW 6 on GPUs and CPUs, written with KernelAbstractions. It reads MF6 input files, writes MF6 head, budget and listing files, and can be run one time step at a time for coupling.\n\nLoad the package of your GPU next to it to enable that backend (using AMDGPU, ModflowGPU; also CUDA, oneAPI, Metal, OpenCL). The exported names cover:\n\nrunning a simulation: run_simulation, load_simulation;\nbackends and precision: select_backend, resolve_precision;\nthe stepping API: initialize, do_time_step, finalize_simulation, get_value, set_value, ...;\nbuilding models in Julia: build_dis, ..., build_simulation, write_simulation;\nreading output files: open_head_file, open_budget_file, read_grb, read_listing_budgets.\n\nOther documented names are public but not exported, because their names are generic (ModflowGPU.run, ModflowGPU.finalize, ModflowGPU.solve, ModflowGPU.update) or they are for backend authors (ModflowGPU.register_backend!). BMI is implemented as methods of BasicModelInterface's functions (BasicModelInterface.update(sim)).\n\nExample\n\nusing ModflowGPU\nresult = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\"; backend = select_backend(\"auto\"))\nresult.converged\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.ModflowGPU"},{"category":"section","location":"compatibility/differences/#Differences-from-MODFLOW-6","page":"Differences from MODFLOW 6","text":"This page is not written yet. It will cover behaviour that differs from MODFLOW 6 on purpose.\n\nUntil then, the developer notes describe the current behaviour.","title":"Differences from MODFLOW 6"},{"category":"section","location":"api/running/#Running-a-simulation","page":"Running a simulation","text":"Run an MF6 simulation directory from start to end with run_simulation. It returns a RunResult with the final heads and the budget of every time step, and writes the output files that the OC package of the input asks for. MF6 input is read by load_simulation, which run_simulation calls for you; call it yourself to inspect or change a simulation before running it.\n\nusing ModflowGPU\nresult = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\"; backend = select_backend(\"auto\"))\nresult.converged\npercent_discrepancy(result.budgets[end])","title":"Running a simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.run_simulation","page":"Running a simulation","text":"run_simulation(simdir::AbstractString; kwargs...) -> RunResult\nrun_simulation(spec::SimulationSpec; kwargs...) -> RunResult\n\nRun the MF6 simulation in directory simdir (the directory with mfsim.nam), or a loaded or built SimulationSpec, from start to end. The simulation has one GWF model and one IMS solution. Each time step applies the stress-period data, solves the heads with the outer (Picard) iterations, computes flows and the budget, and writes the output that the OC package asks for: the head file, the budget file and the model listing file (budget tables), under the names given in the input.\n\nKeywords:\n\nbackend = CPU(): the KernelAbstractions backend, e.g. from select_backend;\nprecision = :auto: a precision preset, see resolve_precision (:auto is :mixed on backends with Float64, else :single);\noutdir = simdir: the directory for the output files (spec.simdir for a spec);\nwrite_output = true: false writes no files;\nclosures = nothing: a NamedTuple that overrides IMS settings, with any of outer_dvclose, outer_maximum, inner_dvclose, inner_rclose, inner_maximum (closures in the model's units); in :single, closures below what Float32 can reach are raised (logged);\npreconditioner = :auto: the linear-solver preconditioner, replacing the ILU0, MILU0 or ILUT of IMS (a ModflowGPU option, not in MF6 input): :auto (:mc_ilu0), :jacobi, :chebyshev, :mc_sgs, :mc_ilu0 or :amg;\nprecond_options = (;): keywords for the preconditioner: degree for :chebyshev, relax for :mc_sgs and :mc_ilu0, the AMG options for :amg (developer notes, docs/dev/mf6_notes.md).\n\nReturns a RunResult with the heads, the budgets and the iteration counts. A time step that does not converge throws a ConvergenceError after writing its output, unless the simulation has the CONTINUE option. Invalid or unsupported input throws an ModflowGPU.MF6InputError or an MF6UnsupportedError before anything runs.\n\nModflowGPU.run is the same function under its earlier name.\n\nExample\n\nusing ModflowGPU\nbackend = select_backend(\"auto\")\nresult = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\"; backend)\nresult.converged\n\n# on a GPU without Float64, or to save memory:\nresult = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\"; backend, precision = :single)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.run_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.run","page":"Running a simulation","text":"ModflowGPU.run(simdir; kwargs...) -> RunResult\n\nThe same function as run_simulation, under the name used before it was exported. Not exported, because it would clash with Base.run; it is kept (not deprecated) for code that calls ModflowGPU.run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.run"},{"category":"type","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.RunResult","page":"Running a simulation","text":"RunResult\n\nWhat run_simulation returns. Fields:\n\nconverged::Bool: every time step converged;\nheads::Vector{Float64}: the absolute heads at the end of the run, per model node (cells with IDOMAIN > 0, in MF6's reduced node order), in the model's length units;\nsteps::Vector{StepResult}: per time step its StepResult (time, iterations);\nbudgets::Vector{ModelBudget}: per time step the volumetric budget (ModelBudget);\nhead_file, budget_file, listing_file: the paths of the output files (\"\" when not written);\nT, Ts, Tp: the precision types used (see resolve_precision);\nmodel: the model, still on the device (internal; its memory is released by the garbage collector).\n\nExample\n\nresult = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\")\nresult.converged\nresult.budgets[end].terms   # budget terms of the last time step\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.RunResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.ModelBudget","page":"Running a simulation","text":"ModelBudget\n\nThe volumetric budget of a model for one time step, as in MF6's listing file: kstp and kper (time step and stress period numbers) and the terms (BudgetTerm) in MF6's order (STO-SS, STO-SY when ICONVERT is used, then the stress packages). Computed on the device in every time step, also when no budget is printed or saved; found in RunResult.budgets and Simulation.budgets.\n\npercent_discrepancy(b) and cum_percent_discrepancy(b) give MF6's percent discrepancy of the rates and of the cumulative volumes.\n\nExample\n\nb = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\").budgets[end]\n[(t.name, t.rate_in, t.rate_out) for t in b.terms]\npercent_discrepancy(b)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.ModelBudget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.BudgetTerm","page":"Running a simulation","text":"BudgetTerm\n\nOne row of a ModelBudget. Fields:\n\nname: the budget text, e.g. \"STO-SS\", \"CHD\", \"RCHA\";\npackage: the package name as in the listing file, e.g. \"STORAGE\", \"CHD_0\";\nrate_in, rate_out: the inflow and outflow rates of the time step (non-negative, Float64, volume per time, L³/T in the model's units);\ncum_in, cum_out: the cumulative inflow and outflow volumes since the start (L³).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.BudgetTerm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.cum_percent_discrepancy","page":"Running a simulation","text":"cum_percent_discrepancy(b::ModelBudget) -> Float64\n\nMF6's percent discrepancy (see percent_discrepancy) of the cumulative volumes of budget b since the start of the simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.cum_percent_discrepancy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.ConvergenceError","page":"Running a simulation","text":"ConvergenceError(kper, kstp, iterations)\n\nThrown by run_simulation (and BasicModelInterface.update) when time step kstp of stress period kper did not converge within OUTER_MAXIMUM (iterations outer iterations) and the simulation has no CONTINUE option. As MF6, the run stops after writing the output of that step.\n\nExample\n\ntry\n    run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\")\ncatch e\n    e isa ConvergenceError || rethrow()\n    @warn \"stopped in stress period $(e.kper), time step $(e.kstp)\"\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.ConvergenceError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.load_simulation","page":"Running a simulation","text":"load_simulation(simdir; strict = true) -> SimulationSpec\n\nRead the MF6 simulation in directory simdir (mfsim.nam, TDIS, the GWF6 models and their packages, GWF6-GWF6 exchanges and IMS6 solutions) into a SimulationSpec, with MF6's checks and error messages (MF6InputError; design and differences in the header of src/input/simulation.jl).\n\nModels other than GWF6, exchanges other than GWF6-GWF6, EMS6 and the out-of-scope packages (UNSUPPORTED_PACKAGES) are always an MF6UnsupportedError (\"unsupported: SFR6 package 'SFR-1' in model 'GWF'\"). With strict = true, so are options, values and features not (yet) implemented by the engine (SUPPORTED_OPTIONS, SUPPORTED_VALUES, SUPPORTED_FEATURES in src/input/supported.jl); strict = false reads all in-scope input (for inspection; the engine may then not run it).\n\nLoading only reads files on the host; nothing is allocated on a device. Pass the result to run_simulation or initialize, or change it and write it back with write_simulation.\n\nExample\n\nspec = load_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\")   # the directory with mfsim.nam\nresult = run_simulation(spec; outdir = \"output\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.load_simulation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.MF6UnsupportedError","page":"Running a simulation","text":"MF6UnsupportedError(file, msg)\n\nInput that ModflowGPU does not support (yet), thrown by load_simulation (and so by run_simulation and initialize) before anything runs. msg starts with \"unsupported: \" and names the package, option or feature; file is the input file (or \"\"). Nothing unsupported is silently ignored.\n\nExample\n\njulia> e = MF6UnsupportedError(\"model.npf\", \"unsupported: NPF option XT3D\");\n\njulia> sprint(showerror, e)\n\"MF6UnsupportedError: unsupported: NPF option XT3D (model.npf)\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.MF6UnsupportedError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/running/#ModflowGPU.MF6InputError","page":"Running a simulation","text":"ModflowGPU.MF6InputError(file, line, msg)\n\nAn error in an MF6 input file, thrown by load_simulation (and so by run_simulation and initialize) with MF6's message where MF6 has one. showerror prints file:line: msg (line = 0 when the error is not about a particular line).\n\nExample\n\njulia> sprint(showerror, ModflowGPU.MF6InputError(\"model.dis\", 12, \"NLAY must be > 0\"))\n\"MF6InputError: model.dis:12: NLAY must be > 0\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.MF6InputError"},{"category":"section","location":"api/backend_authors/#For-backend-authors","page":"For backend authors","text":"ModflowGPU runs on any KernelAbstractions backend. Each GPU package is a weak dependency with a package extension in ext/ (for example ext/AMDGPUExt.jl), which Julia loads when the user loads the GPU package. The extension registers its backend in __init__ and adds methods to the hooks below where the default does not fit its backend:\n\nModflowGPU.register_backend! (required): makes the backend selectable by name;\nsupports_float64: false for devices without Float64 (the default is true);\nModflowGPU.pin_memory!: page-locked host memory for faster transfers;\nModflowGPU.free_memory!: release device memory immediately;\nModflowGPU.host_mapped_zeros: host memory that kernels can write directly.\n\nNone of these are exported; an extension calls them qualified. ModflowGPU.free_device_arrays!, which frees every device array of an object through ModflowGPU.free_memory!, is also useful to coupling code.","title":"For backend authors"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backend_authors/#ModflowGPU.register_backend!","page":"For backend authors","text":"ModflowGPU.register_backend!(name, package, is_functional, construct)\n\nRegister a GPU backend under name (lower case, e.g. \"rocm\"), so that select_backend(name) and select_backend(\"auto\") can find it. package is the name of the GPU package (e.g. \"AMDGPU\"), is_functional a function () -> Bool that tells whether the GPU can be used on this machine, and construct a function () -> KernelAbstractions.Backend. Called by each package extension in its __init__. A later registration under the same name replaces the earlier one.\n\nExample\n\nThe registration of ext/AMDGPUExt.jl:\n\nfunction __init__()\n    return ModflowGPU.register_backend!(\"rocm\", \"AMDGPU\", AMDGPU.functional, ROCBackend)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.register_backend!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backend_authors/#ModflowGPU.pin_memory!","page":"For backend authors","text":"ModflowGPU.pin_memory!(backend, arr)\n\nExtension hook. Page-lock the host array arr to speed up transfers between it and the device. Returns nothing when nothing was done (the default, unless a backend extension adds a method for its backend), otherwise a handle the caller keeps for as long as the memory should stay pinned (CUDA: the array; ROCm: a registered ROCArray wrapper). Copies from pinned memory may run asynchronously: synchronize before overwriting it.\n\nA backend extension adds a method for its backend type.\n\nExample\n\n# in ext/CUDAExt.jl\nModflowGPU.pin_memory!(::CUDABackend, arr) = CUDA.pin(arr)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.pin_memory!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backend_authors/#ModflowGPU.free_memory!","page":"For backend authors","text":"ModflowGPU.free_memory!(backend, arr)\n\nExtension hook. Release the device memory of the device array arr now, instead of when the garbage collector finalises it (two models on one GPU, finalize_simulation). arr must not be used afterwards. The default calls KernelAbstractions.unsafe_free!, which GPU packages implement for their arrays (a no-op for host arrays); extensions add a method where the backend needs something else. Memory shared with other arrays (reshape, unsafe_wrap of the same buffer) is released when the last of them is freed. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.free_memory!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backend_authors/#ModflowGPU.host_mapped_zeros","page":"For backend authors","text":"ModflowGPU.host_mapped_zeros(backend, T, n) -> (device, host) or nothing\n\nA zeroed host vector host of n values of type T and a device array device that aliases its memory (kernels write the host memory directly), or nothing when the backend cannot map host memory (callers then copy with copyto!). Used for the small results of device reductions, which the host reads after every reduction: on ROCm this saves the device-to-host copy (~30 µs on the RX 7800 XT, docs/dev/precision.md). Extension hook: the CPU backend returns the same Array twice, ROCm a registered host array; other backends return nothing unless their extension adds a method.\n\nExample\n\njulia> d, h = ModflowGPU.host_mapped_zeros(select_backend(\"cpu\"), Float64, 2);\n\njulia> d === h\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.host_mapped_zeros"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backend_authors/#ModflowGPU.free_device_arrays!","page":"For backend authors","text":"ModflowGPU.free_device_arrays!(x) -> Int\n\nFree (ModflowGPU.free_memory!) every device array reachable from x through struct fields, array elements and dictionary entries (host arrays of bits types are skipped, wrappers such as views are followed to their parent). Returns the number of arrays freed. Nothing reachable from x may use those arrays afterwards. Used by finalize_simulation, and by coupling code to free its own device buffers before another model allocates.\n\nExample\n\nbuffers = (; recharge = KernelAbstractions.zeros(backend, Float32, n))\nModflowGPU.free_device_arrays!(buffers)   # 1 (0 on the CPU backend)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.free_device_arrays!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/builder/#Building-models","page":"Building models","text":"The builder functions make a SimulationSpec from Julia arrays, with the checks MF6 applies to its input. Grid arrays have the shape (ncol, nrow, nlay) (MF6's memory order); most values may also be a scalar or one value per layer. The result can be run directly with run_simulation or initialize, or written as MF6 input files with write_simulation, which MF6 runs too.\n\nOnly DIS grids can be built so far. Units are those of the model: lengths in its length units (length_units of build_dis), times in its time units (time_units of build_tdis).\n\njulia> dis = build_dis(; delr = 100.0, delc = 100.0, top = 0.0, botm = fill(-10.0, 10, 5, 1));\n\njulia> chd = build_boundary(:CHD, dis; periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, 1, 1), (1, 5, 10)], values = [0.0, -2.0])]);\n\njulia> gwf = build_model(\"flow\"; dis, npf = build_npf(dis; k = 10.0), ic = build_ic(dis; strt = 0.0), boundaries = [chd]);\n\njulia> sim = build_simulation(gwf; tdis = build_tdis(; perlen = 1.0))\nSimulationSpec(1 model(s): FLOW; 1 period(s); 0 exchange(s))","title":"Building models"},{"category":"type","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.SimulationSpec","page":"Building models","text":"SimulationSpec\n\nA whole simulation: simdir (the directory file names are relative to; \"\" for specs built in memory), the mfsim.nam options (continue_on_nonconvergence (CONTINUE), nocheck, memory_print_option, profile_option, maxerrors (0 = MF6 default), print_input), tdis, the GWF models (in mfsim.nam order), exchanges and solution_groups. Look up a model with model_spec(spec, name).\n\nA plain host-side description in Float64, with no device data. It is made by load_simulation (from MF6 input files) or build_simulation (from Julia arrays), and taken by run_simulation, initialize and write_simulation. The specs of its parts (TdisSpec, GwfSpec, DisSpec, ...) are not part of the public API; read them, but build them with the builder functions.\n\nExample\n\nspec = load_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\")\nspec.models[1].name, spec.tdis.perlen\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.SimulationSpec"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_dis","page":"Building models","text":"build_dis(; delr, delc, top, botm, idomain = nothing, xorigin = 0.0, yorigin = 0.0,\n          angrot = 0.0, length_units = :undefined, nogrb = false, grb_file = \"\",\n          file = \"\") -> DisSpec\n\nA structured grid. botm is (ncol, nrow, nlay) (a matrix for one layer); delr is a scalar or a vector of ncol, delc a scalar or a vector of nrow, top a scalar or (ncol, nrow); idomain is nothing (all active), (ncol, nrow) (the same in every layer, as ModflowCoupling's land mask) or (ncol, nrow, nlay). The geometry is checked as MF6 does (check_dis_grid: positive thickness of active cells, at least one active cell); invalid input is an ArgumentError.\n\nLengths (delr, delc, top, botm, origins) are in the model's length units (length_units, e.g. :meters); angrot is in degrees. file is the file name the spec is written to by write_simulation (default <model>.dis).\n\nExample\n\nA grid of 10 columns, 5 rows and 2 layers of 100 m × 100 m cells:\n\njulia> botm = cat(fill(-10.0, 10, 5), fill(-50.0, 10, 5); dims = 3);\n\njulia> dis = build_dis(; delr = 100.0, delc = 100.0, top = 0.0, botm, length_units = :meters);\n\njulia> (dis.ncol, dis.nrow, dis.nlay)\n(10, 5, 2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_dis"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_npf","page":"Building models","text":"build_npf(dis; k, icelltype = 0, k22 = nothing, k33 = nothing, angle1 = nothing,\n          angle2 = nothing, angle3 = nothing, wetdry = nothing, options...) -> NpfSpec\n\nNode property flow on grid dis. Each array is a scalar, one value per layer, a vector by user node or a grid-shaped array ((ncol, nrow, nlay)); nothing means not given (MF6 then uses K22 = K33 = K and zero angles). K, K22 and K33 must be positive in active cells. options are the other NpfSpec fields (cell_averaging, save_flows, ...).\n\nk, k22 and k33 are hydraulic conductivities (L/T, model units); icelltype 0 is a confined cell, other values a convertible cell (as MF6); angles are in degrees.\n\nExample\n\njulia> npf = build_npf(dis; k = [10.0, 1.0], k33 = 0.1, icelltype = [1, 0]);\n\njulia> npf.k[[1, 51]]   # the first cell of each layer\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 10.0\n  1.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_npf"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_ic","page":"Building models","text":"build_ic(dis; strt, file = \"\") -> IcSpec\n\nInitial heads on grid dis (a scalar, one value per layer, by user node or grid-shaped), in the model's length units.\n\nExample\n\njulia> ic = build_ic(dis; strt = fill(-1.0, 10, 5, 1));\n\njulia> length(ic.strt)\n50\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_ic"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_sto","page":"Building models","text":"build_sto(dis; ss, iconvert = 0, sy = nothing, periods = (), options...) -> StoSpec\n\nStorage on grid dis. periods are kper => :steady_state / kper => :transient pairs (MF6's PERIOD blocks; before the first one a model with STO is transient). SS and SY must not be negative in active cells; convertible cells need SY. options are the other StoSpec fields (storagecoefficient, ss_confined_only, save_flows, file).\n\nss is the specific storage (1/L; with storagecoefficient = true the storage coefficient, dimensionless), sy the specific yield (dimensionless).\n\nExample\n\nSteady state in the first stress period, transient from the second on:\n\njulia> sto = build_sto(dis; ss = 1e-5, sy = 0.15, iconvert = 1,\n                       periods = [1 => :steady_state, 2 => :transient]);\n\njulia> sto.sy[1]\n0.15\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_sto"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.oc_record","page":"Building models","text":"oc_record(action, rtype, setting = :all, steps = Int[]) -> OcRecord\noc_record(text) -> OcRecord\n\nOne OC PERIOD record: oc_record(:save, \"HEAD\"), oc_record(:print, \"BUDGET\", :steps, [1, 5]) or oc_record(\"SAVE HEAD FREQUENCY 2\"). action is :save or :print, rtype \"HEAD\" or \"BUDGET\", setting :all, :first, :last, :frequency (one step number) or :steps. Used in the periods of build_oc.\n\nExample\n\njulia> oc_record(:save, \"HEAD\", :frequency, [5]) == oc_record(\"SAVE HEAD FREQUENCY 5\")\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.oc_record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_oc","page":"Building models","text":"build_oc(; head_file = \"\", budget_file = \"\", budgetcsv_file = \"\",\n         head_print_format = nothing, periods = (), file = \"\") -> OcSpec\n\nOutput control. periods are kper => records pairs, the records OcRecords or their text (\"SAVE HEAD ALL\", see oc_record); an empty list turns output off from that period on. head_file and budget_file are the names of the binary output files (none written when empty).\n\nExample\n\nSave heads and budgets at every time step:\n\njulia> oc = build_oc(; head_file = \"model.hds\", budget_file = \"model.cbc\",\n                     periods = [1 => [\"SAVE HEAD ALL\", \"SAVE BUDGET ALL\", \"PRINT BUDGET LAST\"]]);\n\njulia> length(oc.periods.data[1])\n3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_oc"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_boundary","page":"Building models","text":"build_boundary(kind, dis; periods, name = \"\", auxnames = String[], auxmultname = \"\",\n               auxdepthname = \"\", boundnames = false, maxbound = 0, nseg = 1,\n               surf_rate_specified = false, fixed_cell = false, print_input = false,\n               print_flows = false, save_flows = false, file = \"\") -> BoundarySpec{kind}\n\nA list stress package (kind one of BOUNDARY_KINDS) on grid dis. periods are kper => (; cells, values, aux, boundnames, layer) pairs, the records of each PERIOD block (they apply until the next block; an empty cells list removes all stresses):\n\ncells: MF6 cellids (k, i, j), array indices CartesianIndex(j, i, k) (the (ncol, nrow, nlay) shape), CartesianIndex(j, i) with layer (one integer or one per cell; default 1), or user node numbers. Cells must be active.\nvalues: (nrecords, ncolumns), the double columns of the DFN record in order (CHD head; WEL q; RCH recharge; GHB bhead cond; RIV stage cond rbot; DRN elev cond; EVT surface rate depth, then pxdp, petm (nseg - 1 each) and petm0 with surf_rate_specified); a vector when there is one column.\naux: (nrecords, length(auxnames)); boundnames: one name per record (with boundnames = true).\n\nmaxbound = 0 uses the largest number of records of a period. An empty name gets MF6's default (\"WEL-1\", ...) in build_model.\n\nValues are in the model's units: heads, stages and elevations in L, conductances in L²/T, well rates in L³/T (negative for pumping), recharge and evapotranspiration rates in L/T.\n\nExample\n\nTwo constant-head cells and a river cell in stress period 1, the river stage raised in period 2:\n\njulia> chd = build_boundary(:CHD, dis; periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, 1, 1), (1, 5, 10)], values = [0.0, -2.0])]);\n\njulia> riv = build_boundary(:RIV, dis; name = \"river\",\n                            periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, 3, 5)], values = [-1.0 500.0 -3.0]),\n                                       2 => (; cells = [(1, 3, 5)], values = [-0.5 500.0 -3.0])])\nBoundarySpec{:RIV}(\"RIVER\", list, periods [1, 2])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_boundary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_array_boundary","page":"Building models","text":"build_array_boundary(kind, dis; periods, name = \"\", auxnames = String[],\n                     auxmultname = \"\", fixed_cell = false, print_input = false,\n                     print_flows = false, save_flows = false, file = \"\")\n    -> BoundarySpec{kind}\n\nA READASARRAYS stress package (RCHA: kind = :RCH; EVTA: kind = :EVT) on a DIS or DISV grid. periods are kper => (; name = array, ...) pairs: the arrays given in that PERIOD block (recharge, irch; surface, rate, depth, ievt; auxiliary variables by name), each a scalar, (ncol, nrow) or a vector of ncpl. Arrays not given keep their previous values (zero, layer 1 at first), as in MF6. Rates are in L/T in the model's units.\n\nExample\n\nRecharge on the top layer, a uniform rate in period 1 and a map in period 2:\n\njulia> rcha = build_array_boundary(:RCH, dis;\n                                   periods = [1 => (; recharge = 1e-3), 2 => (; recharge = fill(2e-3, 10, 5))])\nBoundarySpec{:RCH}(\"\", arrays, periods [1, 2])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_array_boundary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_model","page":"Building models","text":"build_model(name; dis, npf, ic, sto = nothing, oc = nothing, boundaries = [],\n            list_file = \"\", print_input = false, print_flows = false,\n            save_flows = false, newton = false, newton_under_relaxation = false,\n            file = \"\") -> GwfSpec\n\nA GWF model from package specs (build_dis, build_npf, build_ic, build_sto, build_oc, build_boundary, build_array_boundary). boundaries are given in name-file order; the spec holds them in MF6's order (WEL, DRN, RIV, GHB, RCH, EVT, CHD, then this order). Unnamed stress packages get MF6's default names (\"CHD-1\", ...). Files without a name get <name>.<ext> (lower case; <name>.<package name> for stress packages), the names write_simulation uses. Model names have at most 16 characters (MF6).\n\nExample\n\njulia> chd = build_boundary(:CHD, dis; periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, 1, 1), (1, 5, 10)], values = [0.0, -2.0])]);\n\njulia> wel = build_boundary(:WEL, dis; periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, 3, 5)], values = [-100.0])]);\n\njulia> gwf = build_model(\"flow\"; dis, npf = build_npf(dis; k = 10.0), ic = build_ic(dis; strt = 0.0),\n                         boundaries = [chd, wel])\nGwfSpec(\"FLOW\", DisSpec, boundaries: WEL-1, CHD-1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_tdis","page":"Building models","text":"build_tdis(; perlen, nstp = 1, tsmult = 1.0, time_units = :undefined,\n           start_date_time = \"\", file = \"\") -> TdisSpec\n\nTime discretization: perlen (the length of each stress period, in time_units, e.g. :days); nstp (time steps per period) and tsmult (the ratio of successive step lengths) a scalar (every period) or one value per period. Checked as MF6's check_tdis_timing.\n\nExample\n\nA steady-state period of one day, then ten periods of 30 days with 5 steps each:\n\njulia> tdis = build_tdis(; perlen = [1.0; fill(30.0, 10)], nstp = [1; fill(5, 10)], time_units = :days);\n\njulia> sum(tdis.nstp)\n51\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_tdis"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_ims","page":"Building models","text":"build_ims(; complexity = nothing, models = String[], file = \"\", settings...) -> ImsSpec\n\nAn IMS solution: MF6's defaults of complexity (:simple when not given) with the ImsSpec fields in settings (outer_dvclose = 1e-6, linear_acceleration = :bicgstab, ...). given lists the keywords passed (COMPLEXITY included when given), in the order write_simulation writes them. models is filled in by build_simulation when empty. Closures are in the model's units (head change in L for the dvclose settings).\n\nThe linear-solver preconditioner of IMS (ILU0, MILU0, ILUT) is not used: ModflowGPU replaces it by its own (the preconditioner keyword of run_simulation).\n\nExample\n\njulia> ims = build_ims(; complexity = :moderate, outer_dvclose = 1e-4, linear_acceleration = :bicgstab);\n\njulia> ims.given\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"COMPLEXITY\"\n \"OUTER_DVCLOSE\"\n \"LINEAR_ACCELERATION\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_ims"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.build_simulation","page":"Building models","text":"build_simulation(models; tdis, ims = build_ims(), mxiter = 1, simdir = \"\",\n                 continue_on_nonconvergence = false, nocheck = false,\n                 memory_print_option = \"\", profile_option = \"\", maxerrors = 0,\n                 print_input = false) -> SimulationSpec\n\nA simulation of one or more GWF models (build_model) solved by one IMS solution in one solution group. The IMS solution gets the model names when its models is empty; TDIS and IMS files without a name get <first model>.tdis / .ims. Period data must not refer to stress periods after the last one.\n\nThe result can be run directly (run_simulation, with outdir for the output files) or written as MF6 input (write_simulation).\n\nExample\n\nA one-layer model with two constant heads and a well, in steady state:\n\njulia> dis = build_dis(; delr = 100.0, delc = 100.0, top = 0.0, botm = fill(-10.0, 10, 5, 1));\n\njulia> chd = build_boundary(:CHD, dis; periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, 1, 1), (1, 5, 10)], values = [0.0, -2.0])]);\n\njulia> wel = build_boundary(:WEL, dis; periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, 3, 5)], values = [-100.0])]);\n\njulia> oc = build_oc(; head_file = \"flow.hds\", budget_file = \"flow.cbc\",\n                     periods = [1 => [\"SAVE HEAD ALL\", \"SAVE BUDGET ALL\"]]);\n\njulia> gwf = build_model(\"flow\"; dis, npf = build_npf(dis; k = 10.0), ic = build_ic(dis; strt = 0.0),\n                         oc, boundaries = [chd, wel]);\n\njulia> sim = build_simulation(gwf; tdis = build_tdis(; perlen = 1.0))\nSimulationSpec(1 model(s): FLOW; 1 period(s); 0 exchange(s))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.build_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/builder/#ModflowGPU.write_simulation","page":"Building models","text":"write_simulation(dir, spec::SimulationSpec) -> dir\n\nWrite spec as MF6 input files into dir (created when missing): mfsim.nam and every file under the name in its spec (file fields, PackageEntrys), as build_model and build_simulation set them. load_simulation(dir) gives spec back (spec_identical(spec, load_simulation(dir); ignore = (:simdir,))), and mf6 runs it.\n\nSupported: GWF models on DIS grids with NPF, IC, STO, OC, the stress packages of BOUNDARY_KINDS (lists and READASARRAYS), TDIS and IMS. Exchanges, DISV/DISU, time series, TAS, OBS, TVK, TVS and ATS input are an ArgumentError. Ported from ModflowCoupling's write_simulation (src/mf6_writer.jl there).\n\nExample\n\njulia> dir = write_simulation(mktempdir(), sim);\n\njulia> readdir(dir)\n8-element Vector{String}:\n \"flow.chd-1\"\n \"flow.dis\"\n \"flow.ic\"\n \"flow.ims\"\n \"flow.nam\"\n \"flow.npf\"\n \"flow.tdis\"\n \"mfsim.nam\"\n\njulia> load_simulation(dir)\nSimulationSpec(1 model(s): FLOW; 1 period(s); 0 exchange(s))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.write_simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#GPU-guide","page":"GPU guide","text":"This page explains what changes when a model runs on a GPU: choosing the device, the precision presets and what they mean for the accuracy of heads and budgets, how much GPU memory a model needs, and what to do when something goes wrong. It assumes you have installed a GPU package as described in Installation.\n\nUnless stated otherwise, the numbers on this page were measured on an AMD Radeon RX 7800 XT (16 GB) with ROCm 7.2, on the synthetic regional and global test models of the repository and on the MODFLOW 6 example models. Other GPUs have not been measured.","title":"GPU guide"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Running-on-the-GPU","page":"GPU guide","text":"run_simulation and initialize run on the CPU unless you pass a GPU backend. Load the GPU package, select the backend and pass it on:\n\nusing AMDGPU, ModflowGPU      # needs an AMD GPU; CUDA, oneAPI, Metal or OpenCL likewise\n\nbackend = select_backend(\"auto\")\nresult = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\"; backend)\n\nEverything else stays the same: the input files, the output files and the RunResult. The model is read on the CPU, copied to the GPU once, and every iteration runs on the GPU; heads and budgets are copied back only when the output control asks for them. The listing file states the backend and the precision in its header, for example BACKEND: ROCBackend, PRECISION: T = Float64, Ts = Float64, Tp = Float32.\n\nOn the AMD GPU, heads were bitwise identical to those of the CPU backend on the test models, in every precision.","title":"Running on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Precision-presets","page":"GPU guide","text":"A GPU computes much faster in single precision (Float32, about 7 significant digits) than in double precision (Float64, about 16 digits), and needs half the memory per value. Some GPUs have no Float64 at all. ModflowGPU therefore has four precision presets, chosen with the precision keyword:\n\nPreset Heads and flows Linear solver Preconditioner Needs Float64\n:double Float64 Float64 Float64 yes\n:mixed Float64 Float64 Float32 yes\n:single Float32 Float32 Float32 no\n:auto (default) :mixed where the backend has Float64, else :single   \n\nresolve_precision shows what a preset means on a backend. MODFLOW 6 itself always computes in Float64.","title":"Precision presets"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#What-the-presets-mean-for-accuracy","page":"GPU guide","text":":double and :mixed give the accuracy of MODFLOW 6. Only the preconditioner, which speeds up the linear solver but does not decide the answer, is stored in Float32 in :mixed. With tight solver closures, heads agree with MODFLOW 6 within 4e-8 m on the test models, and within 4.4e-11 m on the real global model (3.7 million cells, 31 daily steps), where the budget terms agree to 1e-12 relative.\n:single gives heads within 2e-4 m of MODFLOW 6 on most test models, and within 6e-4 m on all of them (the largest, ex-gwf-advtidal, has convertible cells and tides). On the real global model the largest head difference after 31 daily steps was 2.5e-5 m. Percent discrepancies are mostly below 0.003 %, but see the budget item below.\n\nFloat32 stores about 7 digits, so its error grows with the size of the numbers stored. ModflowGPU stores each head as the difference from a reference head of its cell (normally the starting head), which keeps those numbers small. The error of a :single head is therefore roughly 1e-7 times how far the head moves away from its starting head: 1e-4 m for a head that moves 1000 m, much less for most models.\n\nWhat you will notice in :single:\n\nThe closures have a floor. Float32 cannot resolve head changes below a certain size, so OUTERDVCLOSE and INNERDVCLOSE below 1e-6 m (or below 1e-7 times the largest head offset) are raised, with a logged message such as Float32 run: OUTER_DVCLOSE 1.0e-9 and INNER_DVCLOSE 1.0e-10 raised to at least 1.0e-6 and 1.0e-6 (FLOAT32_CLOSURES). INNER_RCLOSE gets a similar floor. The model then often converges in fewer outer iterations than in MODFLOW 6.\nSteady states can stop short. On a badly conditioned steady state (a regional spin-up of the Indus basin with real inputs) the :single heads stopped 3 to 7 cm from the converged Float64 heads, whatever the preconditioner and closure. Run spin-ups and other long steady states in :mixed (the :auto default where Float64 exists).\nSome models do not converge with the default preconditioner in :single. On two of the MODFLOW 6 examples (ex-gwf-lgrv-gr, ex-gwf-hwell-a with the Jacobi preconditioner) the run fails with a ConvergenceError; preconditioner = :amg or :mixed fixes it.\nBudgets with very large coefficients are rounded coarsely. A flow is computed from Float32 heads, so where a very large conductance or storage coefficient meets a small flow, the rounding of the heads shows in the flow. On a radial test grid with conductances of 5.5e7 ft²/d next to the well, single FLOW-JA-FACE values differed by up to 2e-4 of the total inflow, while the heads agreed within 1.5e-6 ft. On three of the MODFLOW 6 example models with convertible cells, the :single percent discrepancy reached 0.02 % to 48 % (the largest on ex-gwf-rad-disu), while the heads were fine. Use :mixed where budgets and cell-by-cell flows matter.","title":"What the presets mean for accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Which-preset-to-use","page":"GPU guide","text":"Situation Preset\nAny model, on a GPU or CPU with Float64 (AMD, NVIDIA, the CPU) :auto (= :mixed)\nA GPU without Float64 (Apple, some Intel) :auto (= :single; the only option)\nTransient runs where speed matters more than the last 0.1 mm, after checking a run against :mixed :single\nComparing with MODFLOW 6 to the last digit, or checking that :mixed is not the cause of a difference :double\n\nSpeed, on the synthetic global model (6.6 million cells, a steady state and 30 daily steps, default preconditioner): :double 9.6 s, :mixed 8.7 s, :single 3.6 s in total (benchmark/results/2026-11-16-T4.7.md). On the CPU backend :mixed and :double take about the same time. More numbers are on the Performance page.","title":"Which preset to use"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Preconditioner","page":"GPU guide","text":"The linear solver uses CG or BiCGSTAB as IMS asks, but not IMS's ILU0, MILU0 or ILUT preconditioner: those do not run in parallel on a GPU. ModflowGPU replaces it with its own, chosen with the preconditioner keyword:\n\npreconditioner What it is When to use it\n:auto (default) = :mc_ilu0 ILU0 in multicolour order almost always: the fastest over a whole run on every benchmark model, backend and precision\n:amg algebraic multigrid badly conditioned steady states, models that do not converge with :mc_ilu0 (needs about 20 % more memory)\n:jacobi diagonal scaling least memory; needs many more iterations\n:mc_sgs, :chebyshev multicolour Gauss–Seidel, Chebyshev polynomial for experiments\n\nprecond_options passes settings to the preconditioner: relax for :mc_ilu0 (the MILU relaxation factor, IMS RELAXATION_FACTOR, which is not taken from the IMS file), degree for :chebyshev, and the AMG settings for :amg. The defaults are a good start.\n\nBecause the preconditioner differs, the number of inner iterations differs from MODFLOW 6. The heads still agree within the closure criteria.","title":"Preconditioner"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Memory","page":"GPU guide","text":"The whole model lives on the GPU: the matrix, the solver vectors, the preconditioner and the boundary data. Peak GPU memory per million active cells, measured on the synthetic global model (6.6 million cells in 2 layers, with a river or a drain, recharge and evapotranspiration on every land cell, benchmark/results/2026-11-08-T4.4.md):\n\nPreconditioner :double :mixed :single\n:mc_ilu0 (default) 0.57 GiB 0.54 GiB 0.37 GiB\n:amg 0.69 GiB 0.65 GiB 0.46 GiB\n:jacobi 0.46 GiB 0.45 GiB 0.29 GiB\n\nLater solver changes added about 0.06 GiB per million cells: with the default preconditioner the same model now peaks at 4.2 GiB in :double, 4.0 GiB in :mixed and 2.8 GiB in :single (benchmark/results/2026-11-16-T6.1.md), 0.64, 0.61 and 0.43 GiB per million cells. As a rule of thumb, plan for about 0.65 GiB per million cells in :mixed and 0.45 GiB in :single; in :mixed the synthetic global model uses a quarter of a 16 GB card. Models with more connections per cell (DISV, DISU) or many more boundary records need more.","title":"Memory"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Sharing-the-GPU-with-another-model","page":"GPU guide","text":"ModflowGPU can share one GPU, in one Julia process, with another model that is also written with KernelAbstractions, such as the mGV hydrology model in ModflowCoupling. This was tested with mGV on the AMD GPU: a one-year daily coupled run of the Indus basin, with mGV using 64 MiB and ModflowGPU 23 MiB of GPU memory. Things to know:\n\nBoth models must use the same versions of KernelAbstractions and the GPU package, so they must be in one Julia environment that resolves.\nSelect the backend once and pass the same backend to both models.\nfinalize_simulation releases the GPU memory of a simulation at once (with free = true, the default), instead of when Julia's garbage collector runs. Call it before the other model allocates a lot of memory.\nThe stepping API can hand device arrays to the other model (get_value_ptr(sim, address; device = true)), so data need not pass through the CPU.\n\nTwo separate Julia processes on one GPU have not been tested.","title":"Sharing the GPU with another model"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Troubleshooting","page":"GPU guide","text":"","title":"Troubleshooting"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#The-first-run-is-slow","page":"GPU guide","text":"Julia compiles the code of ModflowGPU for your backend and precision the first time it is used. On the CPU of the development machine (Ryzen 7 7700) the first run of the small model of Getting started takes about 20 s, the second less than a second. A run in another precision or with another preconditioner compiles again, a few seconds each. Keep one Julia session open and run several models in it, instead of starting Julia for every run; the compiled code is kept for as long as the session runs. Loading the package (using ModflowGPU) takes a few seconds more.","title":"The first run is slow"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#does-not-support-Float64","page":"GPU guide","text":"precision = :double was requested, but backend MetalBackend does not support Float64. Use precision = :single instead.\n\nThe GPU has no Float64 (Apple GPUs, some Intel GPUs and OpenCL devices), so :double and :mixed cannot run. Use precision = :single, or leave the default :auto, which picks :single there.","title":"does not support Float64"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Out-of-GPU-memory","page":"GPU guide","text":"The GPU package throws an out-of-memory error when the model does not fit. Check the memory table above, then:\n\nuse precision = :single (about 30 % less memory), or preconditioner = :jacobi;\ncall finalize_simulation on simulations you no longer need, and close other programs that use the GPU;\nrun on the CPU (backend = select_backend(\"cpu\")), which is slower but limited only by the computer's memory.","title":"Out of GPU memory"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#Scalar-indexing-is-disallowed","page":"GPU guide","text":"This error comes from the GPU package when Julia code reads or writes a GPU array one element at a time, which would be extremely slow. ModflowGPU never does this itself; it happens when you index a device array, for example one from get_value_ptr(sim, address; device = true), outside a kernel. Use the host copy instead (get_value, or get_value_ptr without device = true), copy the whole array with Array(x), or use whole-array operations (x .+= 1, sum(x)), which run on the GPU.","title":"Scalar indexing is disallowed"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#The-run-stops-with-a-ConvergenceError","page":"GPU guide","text":"ConvergenceError: the solution did not converge in time step 1 of stress period 1 (500 outer iterations); add CONTINUE to the mfsim.nam OPTIONS to continue\n\nAs in MODFLOW 6, a time step that does not converge within OUTER_MAXIMUM stops the run after its output is written, unless mfsim.nam has the CONTINUE option. If the same input converges in MODFLOW 6:\n\nin :single, try :mixed (on a GPU with Float64) or preconditioner = :amg;\nwith the default preconditioner, try preconditioner = :amg;\nif the log shows the correction or the heads are not finite (the linear solver diverged), the linear solver failed: the same two remedies apply.","title":"The run stops with a ConvergenceError"},{"category":"section","location":"gpu/#The-CPU-run-is-slow","page":"GPU guide","text":"The CPU backend runs the same code as the GPU, on the CPU threads Julia was started with. Start Julia with --threads=auto. The CPU backend is meant for testing and for small models; on large models a GPU is many times faster (see Performance).","title":"The CPU run is slow"},{"category":"section","location":"coupling/stepping/#Stepping-API","page":"Stepping API","text":"This page is not written yet. It will cover the time-step loop, variable addresses, and host and device arrays for coupling.\n\nUntil then, the Stepping API reference lists the functions, and the developer notes describe the current behaviour.","title":"Stepping API"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"This page is not written yet. It will cover what speed-up to expect and when a GPU helps.\n\nUntil then, the developer notes describe the current behaviour.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This page runs a small MODFLOW 6 simulation with ModflowGPU, reads its results back, and shows the options that matter most. Everything on it runs on the CPU, so you can follow it right after Installation, without a GPU and without downloading a model. The same steps work for your own simulation: replace the folder by the one that holds your mfsim.nam.\n\nThe code blocks are meant to be typed into the Julia REPL (started with julia --project=. in the folder of your environment). Lines starting with julia> are input; the lines below them are what Julia prints.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#A-small-model-to-run","page":"Getting started","text":"ModflowGPU reads ordinary MODFLOW 6 input files. To have a simulation to run, we build one in Julia with the builder functions and write it as MF6 input files with write_simulation. If you already have a simulation folder, skip to the next section.\n\nThe model is a confined aquifer of 20 columns, 10 rows and 2 layers of 100 m × 100 m cells: an upper layer from 0 to −10 m with K = 10 m/d and a lower layer from −10 to −50 m with K = 1 m/d. A river or lake on the west edge holds the head at 0 m (CHD in column 1 of layer 1), 1 mm/d of recharge falls on the other cells of layer 1, and a well pumps 500 m³/d from layer 2. One steady-state stress period.\n\njulia> using ModflowGPU\n\njulia> botm = cat(fill(-10.0, 20, 10), fill(-50.0, 20, 10); dims = 3);  # (ncol, nrow, nlay)\n\njulia> dis = build_dis(; delr = 100.0, delc = 100.0, top = 0.0, botm, length_units = :meters);\n\njulia> npf = build_npf(dis; k = [10.0, 1.0]);  # one value per layer\n\njulia> chd = build_boundary(:CHD, dis;\n           periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, i, 1) for i in 1:10], values = zeros(10))]);\n\njulia> rch = build_boundary(:RCH, dis;\n           periods = [1 => (; cells = [(1, i, j) for i in 1:10 for j in 2:20], values = fill(1.0e-3, 190))]);\n\njulia> wel = build_boundary(:WEL, dis; periods = [1 => (; cells = [(2, 5, 15)], values = [-500.0])]);\n\njulia> oc = build_oc(; head_file = \"flow.hds\", budget_file = \"flow.cbc\",\n           periods = [1 => [\"SAVE HEAD ALL\", \"SAVE BUDGET ALL\", \"PRINT BUDGET ALL\"]]);\n\njulia> gwf = build_model(\"flow\"; dis, npf, ic = build_ic(dis; strt = 0.0), oc, boundaries = [chd, wel, rch],\n           save_flows = true);\n\njulia> sim = build_simulation(gwf; tdis = build_tdis(; perlen = 1.0, time_units = :days));\n\njulia> simdir = write_simulation(joinpath(mktempdir(), \"flow\"), sim);\n\njulia> readdir(simdir)\n11-element Vector{String}:\n \"flow.chd-1\"\n \"flow.dis\"\n \"flow.ic\"\n \"flow.ims\"\n \"flow.nam\"\n \"flow.npf\"\n \"flow.oc\"\n \"flow.rch-1\"\n \"flow.tdis\"\n \"flow.wel-1\"\n \"mfsim.nam\"\n\nCells are given as MF6 cell ids (layer, row, column). Grid arrays such as botm have the shape (ncol, nrow, nlay), the order in which MF6 stores them. save_flows = true is the SAVE_FLOWS option of the model name file: without it, as in MODFLOW 6, no flows are written to the budget file. simdir is now an ordinary MF6 simulation folder; mf6 runs it too.","title":"A small model to run"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Run-the-simulation","page":"Getting started","text":"run_simulation reads mfsim.nam and the files it lists, runs every time step, and writes the output that the OC package asks for into the same folder:\n\njulia> result = run_simulation(simdir);\n┌ Info: IMS preconditioner ILU0 is replaced by mc_ilu0 (multicolour ILU0: red–black on DIS grids, greedy colouring on DISV and DISU; the default, see the `preconditioner` option)\n└   ims.file = \"flow.ims\"\n\njulia> result.converged\ntrue\n\njulia> filter(f -> endswith(f, r\"\\.(hds|cbc|lst)\"), readdir(simdir))\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"flow.cbc\"\n \"flow.hds\"\n \"flow.lst\"\n\nThe first run takes a while (about 20 s on a desktop CPU), because Julia compiles the code first; a second run in the same Julia session takes less than a second. The Info message says that the linear-solver preconditioner of IMS was replaced; see Options below.\n\nThe output files have the names given in the input:\n\nflow.hds, the head file, and flow.cbc, the budget file, as asked by the SAVE HEAD and SAVE BUDGET records of OC. They are MF6 binary files in the same format as MODFLOW 6 writes.\nflow.lst, the listing file of the model, with the volumetric budget tables (as asked by PRINT BUDGET) and the iteration counts of each time step. It is shorter than MODFLOW 6's listing file: it does not echo the input.\n\nTo write the output somewhere else, pass outdir = \"path/to/output\"; to write nothing, write_output = false.","title":"Run the simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Read-the-results","page":"Getting started","text":"","title":"Read the results"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#In-Julia","page":"Getting started","text":"The RunResult that run_simulation returns already holds the final heads and the budget of every time step:\n\njulia> length(result.heads)   # one head per active cell, in MF6's node order\n400\n\njulia> for t in result.budgets[end].terms   # the budget of the last time step\n           println(rpad(t.package, 7), \"in: \", round(t.rate_in; digits = 1), \"  out: \", round(t.rate_out; digits = 1))\n       end\nWEL-1  in: 0.0  out: 500.0\nRCH-1  in: 1900.0  out: 0.0\nCHD-1  in: 0.0  out: 1400.0\n\njulia> abs(percent_discrepancy(result.budgets[end])) < 0.01\ntrue\n\nRates are in the model's units, here m³/d. The output files can be read with the readers of ModflowGPU, which read MODFLOW 6's own output files as well. A head file holds one record per saved time step and layer:\n\njulia> hf = open_head_file(joinpath(simdir, \"flow.hds\"));\n\njulia> h = read_head(hf, length(hf));   # the last saved heads\n\njulia> size(h)   # (ncol, nrow, nlay)\n(20, 10, 2)\n\njulia> round(h[15, 5, 2]; digits = 2)   # the well cell: column 15, row 5, layer 2\n5.9\n\njulia> round(maximum(h); digits = 2)    # the highest head, at the east edge\n8.69\n\nNote the order of the indices: h[column, row, layer]. The budget file has one record per budget term and saved time step:\n\njulia> bf = open_budget_file(joinpath(simdir, \"flow.cbc\"));\n\njulia> budget_texts(bf)\n4-element Vector{String}:\n \"FLOW-JA-FACE\"\n \"WEL\"\n \"RCH\"\n \"CHD\"\n\njulia> wel = read_budget(bf; text = \"WEL\")[end];   # the last saved time step\n\njulia> wel.id1, wel.q   # cell (node number) and flow, negative out of the model\n(Int32[295], [-500.0])\n\nThe budget tables of the listing file can be read too, with read_listing_budgets:\n\njulia> tables = read_listing_budgets(joinpath(simdir, \"flow.lst\"));\n\njulia> [(t.name, round(t.rate_in), round(t.rate_out)) for t in tables[end].terms]   # m³/d\n3-element Vector{Tuple{String, Float64, Float64}}:\n (\"WEL\", 0.0, 500.0)\n (\"RCH\", 1900.0, 0.0)\n (\"CHD\", 0.0, 1400.0)","title":"In Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#In-Python-with-FloPy","page":"Getting started","text":"The head and budget files are MF6 binary files, so FloPy reads them as it reads MODFLOW 6's:\n\nimport flopy\n\nhds = flopy.utils.HeadFile(\"flow/flow.hds\")\nh = hds.get_data()                    # last saved heads, shape (nlay, nrow, ncol)\n\ncbc = flopy.utils.CellBudgetFile(\"flow/flow.cbc\")\nwel = cbc.get_data(text=\"WEL\")[-1]    # record array with node, node2 and q\n\nFloPy's arrays are (nlay, nrow, ncol) with indices from 0, so Julia's h[15, 5, 2] is FloPy's h[1, 4, 14].","title":"In Python with FloPy"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Options","page":"Getting started","text":"The keywords of run_simulation (and of initialize for the stepping API) that you are most likely to need:\n\nKeyword Default What it does\nbackend the CPU where the model runs: select_backend(\"auto\") for the first GPU that works\nprecision :auto :double, :mixed or :single; :auto is :mixed where the backend has Float64, else :single\npreconditioner :auto (= :mc_ilu0) the preconditioner that replaces IMS's ILU0, MILU0 or ILUT: :mc_ilu0, :amg, :jacobi, :mc_sgs, :chebyshev\nprecond_options none settings of the preconditioner, e.g. (; relax = 0.97) for MILU with :mc_ilu0\noutdir the input folder where the output files go\nwrite_output true false writes no output files\nclosures the IMS values override IMS closures, e.g. (; outer_dvclose = 1e-4, inner_maximum = 200)\n\nNote that the default backend is the CPU: to run on a GPU, pass backend = select_backend(\"auto\") (after loading the GPU package, see the GPU guide).\n\nThe same model in single precision, without writing output:\n\njulia> backend = select_backend(\"cpu\");   # select_backend(\"auto\") to use a GPU\n\njulia> r32 = run_simulation(simdir; backend, precision = :single, write_output = false);\n┌ Info: IMS preconditioner ILU0 is replaced by mc_ilu0 (multicolour ILU0: red–black on DIS grids, greedy colouring on DISV and DISU; the default, see the `preconditioner` option)\n└   ims.file = \"flow.ims\"\n\njulia> r32.T, r32.Ts, r32.Tp   # state, linear solver, preconditioner\n(Float32, Float32, Float32)\n\njulia> maximum(abs.(r32.heads .- result.heads)) < 1e-4   # metres\ntrue","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Which-options-should-I-pick?","page":"Getting started","text":"Start with the defaults: precision = :auto and preconditioner = :auto. On a GPU with Float64 (AMD, NVIDIA) and on the CPU that is :mixed with :mc_ilu0, which gives MODFLOW 6's accuracy and was the fastest combination with that accuracy on every benchmark model. Change them only for a reason:\n\nIf ... then use because\nyou want the fastest transient run and can accept heads about 1e-4 m less accurate precision = :single about 2.5 times faster than :mixed on a 6.6 million cell model, about 30 % less memory\nthe GPU has no Float64 (Apple, some Intel) the default (:auto gives :single) :double and :mixed cannot run there\nyou run a long steady state (spin-up) the default :mixed, not :single in :single such steady states can stop centimetres short of the solution\na time step does not converge (ConvergenceError), especially in :single preconditioner = :amg, or :mixed AMG converges on badly conditioned steady states where :mc_ilu0 does not\nthe model does not fit in GPU memory precision = :single, or preconditioner = :jacobi see the memory table in the GPU guide\nyou compare with MODFLOW 6 to the last digit precision = :double Float64 everywhere, as MODFLOW 6\n\nThe GPU guide explains the presets, their accuracy and memory use in more detail.","title":"Which options should I pick?"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Unsupported-input","page":"Getting started","text":"ModflowGPU never ignores input silently. Input that it does not support stops the run with an MF6UnsupportedError that names the file, the package and the option, before anything runs. For example, after adding the XT3D option (not supported yet) to the NPF file:\n\njulia> npf_file = joinpath(simdir, \"flow.npf\");\n\njulia> original = read(npf_file, String);\n\njulia> write(npf_file, replace(original, \"BEGIN OPTIONS\" => \"BEGIN OPTIONS\\n  XT3D\"));\n\njulia> run_simulation(simdir)\nERROR: MF6UnsupportedError: unsupported: NPF6 option XT3D (model 'FLOW') (/tmp/jl_A1b2C3/flow/flow.npf)\n[...]\n\nPackages outside the scope of ModflowGPU (SFR, LAK, MAW, UZF, MVR, CSUB, the transport models, ...) give the same error. Supported packages lists what is supported.\n\nReading is strict by default. load_simulation with strict = false reads all input that ModflowGPU can parse, without checking it against what the engine supports, so that you can inspect or change a simulation in Julia (and write it back with write_simulation). The engine still refuses what it cannot compute, when the run starts:\n\njulia> spec = load_simulation(simdir; strict = false)\nSimulationSpec(1 model(s): FLOW; 1 period(s); 0 exchange(s))\n\njulia> run_simulation(spec; write_output = false)\nERROR: MF6UnsupportedError: unsupported: NPF6 option XT3D (flow.npf)\n[...]\n\njulia> write(npf_file, original);   # restore the NPF file","title":"Unsupported input"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#IMS-options-that-are-replaced","page":"Getting started","text":"Some IMS options are accepted but work differently in ModflowGPU. They are reported in the log when the run starts (most of them once per Julia session), as an Info when nothing you set yourself is lost, or as a Warning when it is. A simulation with COMPLEXITY COMPLEX, for example:\n\njulia> ims = build_ims(; complexity = :complex);\n\njulia> sim_complex = build_simulation(gwf; tdis = build_tdis(; perlen = 1.0, time_units = :days), ims);\n\njulia> r = run_simulation(sim_complex; write_output = false);\n┌ Warning: IMS BACKTRACKING_NUMBER = 20 (COMPLEXITY COMPLEX or given): backtracking is not implemented yet; outer iterations may differ from MF6\n│   ims.file = \"flow.ims\"\n└ @ ModflowGPU ~/ModflowGPU/src/solver/picard.jl:77\n┌ Info: IMS preconditioner ILUT is replaced by mc_ilu0 (multicolour ILU0: red–black on DIS grids, greedy colouring on DISV and DISU; the default, see the `preconditioner` option)\n└   ims.file = \"flow.ims\"\n\njulia> r.converged\ntrue\n\n(A SimulationSpec built in Julia can be run directly, without writing files.)\n\nWhat these messages mean:\n\nThe preconditioner. IMS's ILU0, MILU0 and ILUT (set by PRECONDITIONERLEVELS, RELAXATIONFACTOR or COMPLEXITY) are replaced by the preconditioner option, because they do not run in parallel. This changes the number of inner iterations, not the converged heads. With the default preconditioner = :auto it is an Info for ILU0 and for preconditioners that come from COMPLEXITY alone, and a Warning when you set RELAXATIONFACTOR or PRECONDITIONERLEVELS yourself, because their values are not used (when you choose preconditioner yourself it is always an Info): for example IMS preconditioner MILU0 is replaced by mc_ilu0 (...). Pass precond_options = (; relax = 0.97) to use MILU relaxation with :mc_ilu0.\nBacktracking (BACKTRACKING_NUMBER > 0, also set by COMPLEXITY COMPLEX) is not implemented: a Warning. The outer iterations then differ from MODFLOW 6's, but the converged heads agree within the closure criteria.\nREORDERING_METHOD has no effect with ModflowGPU's preconditioners: an Info.\nIn :single, closures below what Float32 can resolve are raised: an Info. See the GPU guide.","title":"IMS options that are replaced"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting started","text":"GPU guide: run on a GPU, precision, memory and troubleshooting.\nSupported packages: check that your model is supported.\nStepping API: run the model one time step at a time and change its boundary data from Julia.\nAPI reference: every function and its options.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"compatibility/packages/#Supported-packages","page":"Supported packages","text":"This page is not written yet. It will cover which MODFLOW 6 packages, blocks and options are supported, and which are not.\n\nUntil then, the developer notes describe the current behaviour.","title":"Supported packages"},{"category":"section","location":"api/backends/#Backends-and-precision","page":"Backends and precision","text":"A backend is the device a simulation runs on, as a KernelAbstractions Backend. GPU backends become available when their package is loaded next to ModflowGPU (for example using AMDGPU, ModflowGPU); select_backend picks one by name. The precision preset sets the floating-point types of the model state, the linear solver and the preconditioner; resolve_precision shows what a preset means on a backend.\n\njulia> backend = select_backend(\"cpu\");\n\njulia> resolve_precision(backend, :auto)\n(T = Float64, Ts = Float64, Tp = Float32)","title":"Backends and precision"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backends/#ModflowGPU.select_backend","page":"Backends and precision","text":"select_backend(name=\"auto\")\n\nSelect the KernelAbstractions backend to run the model on. This is evaluated at runtime (not during precompilation), so the same precompiled package can be used on machines with and without a GPU.\n\nname is one of \"auto\", \"cpu\", \"cuda\", \"rocm\", \"metal\", \"oneapi\", \"opencl\". GPU backends are only available once their package is loaded (e.g. using AMDGPU). With \"auto\" the first loaded and functional GPU backend is used, falling back to CPU. Explicitly requesting a GPU backend that is not loaded or not functional throws an error.\n\nA KernelAbstractions.Backend can also be passed directly, which is returned as is.\n\nReturns a KernelAbstractions.Backend, to pass as the backend keyword of run_simulation and initialize. The CPU backend of KernelAbstractions 0.10 is POCLBackend (CPU()); it is meant for tests and small models.\n\nExample\n\njulia> backend = select_backend(\"cpu\");\n\njulia> backend_name(backend)\n\"POCLBackend\"\n\nWith a GPU package loaded:\n\nusing AMDGPU, ModflowGPU\nbackend = select_backend(\"auto\")   # ROCBackend() when the GPU works, else the CPU\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.select_backend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backends/#ModflowGPU.backend_options","page":"Backends and precision","text":"backend_options() -> Vector{String}\n\nAll valid backend names for select_backend: \"auto\", \"cpu\" and the GPU backends, known ones in order of preference for \"auto\" first. A GPU backend is listed even when its package is not loaded; selecting it then throws an error that names the package to load.\n\nExample\n\njulia> backend_options()\n7-element Vector{String}:\n \"auto\"\n \"cpu\"\n \"cuda\"\n \"rocm\"\n \"metal\"\n \"oneapi\"\n \"opencl\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.backend_options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backends/#ModflowGPU.backend_name","page":"Backends and precision","text":"backend_name(backend) -> String\n\nThe name of the type of the KernelAbstractions backend, e.g. \"POCLBackend\" (KA's CPU()), \"ROCBackend\" (AMDGPU) or \"CUDABackend\". Used in log messages and the listing file.\n\nExample\n\njulia> backend_name(select_backend(\"cpu\"))\n\"POCLBackend\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.backend_name"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backends/#ModflowGPU.supports_float64","page":"Backends and precision","text":"supports_float64(backend)::Bool\n\nWhether backend can run kernels in Float64, which the :double and :mixed precision presets need. Extension hook like ModflowGPU.pin_memory!: the default is true (CPU, CUDA, ROCm). The Metal extension returns false, and the oneAPI and OpenCL extensions query the device.\n\nThis is deliberately not KernelAbstractions.supports_float64, whose default (through KernelInterface, since KernelAbstractions 0.10) is false for backends that do not define it.\n\nExample\n\njulia> supports_float64(select_backend(\"cpu\"))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.supports_float64"},{"category":"function","location":"api/backends/#ModflowGPU.resolve_precision","page":"Backends and precision","text":"resolve_precision(backend, preset = :auto) -> (; T, Ts, Tp)\n\nResolve a precision preset to the floating-point types for state and assembly (T), the Krylov solver (Ts) and the preconditioner (Tp):\n\nPreset T Ts Tp\n:double Float64 Float64 Float64\n:mixed Float64 Float64 Float32\n:single Float32 Float32 Float32\n:auto :mixed if supports_float64(backend), else :single  \n\n:mixed keeps the state, assembly and Krylov vectors in Float64 (the :double parity tolerances hold) and stores every preconditioner's data (Jacobi minv, Chebyshev and multicolour operator copies and factors, the AMG hierarchy) in Float32. It is the :auto default on backends with Float64 because it is as accurate as :double and 10–20 % faster with less memory (measured in docs/dev/precision.md).\n\nThe result is a NamedTuple, so T, Ts, Tp = resolve_precision(backend, preset) works. Requesting :double or :mixed on a backend without Float64 support throws an error. preset may also be a string (\"single\").\n\nExample\n\njulia> backend = select_backend(\"cpu\");\n\njulia> resolve_precision(backend)\n(T = Float64, Ts = Float64, Tp = Float32)\n\njulia> resolve_precision(backend, :single)\n(T = Float32, Ts = Float32, Tp = Float32)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.resolve_precision"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/backends/#ModflowGPU.PRECISION_PRESETS","page":"Backends and precision","text":"ModflowGPU.PRECISION_PRESETS\n\nThe precision presets accepted by resolve_precision and the precision keyword of run_simulation: (:auto, :double, :mixed, :single).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.PRECISION_PRESETS"},{"category":"section","location":"api/stepping/#Stepping-API","page":"Stepping API","text":"The stepping API runs a simulation one time step at a time, as libmf6's API (XMI) does, so that another model can read and change its variables between steps. initialize builds the model; ModflowGPU.update runs one time step, or the calls it is made of (prepare_time_step, then do_time_step or prepare_solve, ModflowGPU.solve and finalize_solve, then finalize_time_step); finalize_simulation ends it. Variables are named by libmf6's addresses, such as \"GWF/X\" (heads) or \"GWF/RIV-1/BOUND\" (river data), and are read and written as host arrays in Float64 with absolute heads.\n\nusing ModflowGPU\nsim = initialize(\"path/to/simulation\"; backend = select_backend(\"auto\"))\nriv = get_value_ptr(sim, \"GWF/RIV-1/BOUND\")   # (3, nriv): stage, cond, rbot\nwhile get_current_time(sim) < get_end_time(sim)\n    prepare_time_step(sim)\n    riv[1, :] .= my_river_stages(get_current_time(sim))\n    do_time_step(sim) || @warn \"no convergence\"\n    finalize_time_step(sim)\n    heads = get_value(sim, \"GWF/X\")\nend\nfinalize_simulation(sim)","title":"Stepping API"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.Simulation","page":"Stepping API","text":"Simulation\n\nA simulation driven one time step at a time, created by initialize and used by the other functions of the stepping API. It is also the model type of the BMI methods (BasicModelInterface.initialize(Simulation, dir)).\n\nFields that are useful to read (do not change them):\n\nspec: the SimulationSpec it was built from;\nT, Ts, Tp: the precision types (see resolve_precision);\nsteps: the TDIS time steps, and istep, the index of the current one (0 before the first prepare_time_step);\nresults and budgets: per completed time step its StepResult and ModelBudget.\n\nThe other fields (the model on the device, the solver, the output files, the call stage) are internal.\n\nExample\n\nsim = initialize(\"path/to/simulation\")\nsim.istep, length(sim.steps)   # (0, number of time steps)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.Simulation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.StepResult","page":"Stepping API","text":"StepResult\n\nThe result of one time step, in RunResult.steps and Simulation.results.\n\nFields:\n\nstep: the TDIS time step, with kper, kstp (stress period and time step numbers), delt (step length), pertim and totim (time in the period and in the simulation at the end of the step), in the model's time units;\nouter: the outer-iteration result, with converged::Bool, iterations (outer iterations) and inner (total inner, linear-solver, iterations).\n\nExample\n\nresult = run_simulation(\"path/to/simulation\")\n[(r.step.kper, r.step.kstp, r.outer.iterations) for r in result.steps]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.StepResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.initialize","page":"Stepping API","text":"initialize(simdir::AbstractString; kwargs...) -> Simulation\ninitialize(spec::SimulationSpec; kwargs...) -> Simulation\n\nStart a simulation to be run one time step at a time: load the MF6 simulation in directory simdir (or take a loaded or built spec; one GWF model with one IMS solution), build the model, the outer solver and the budget accumulator on the device, and open the output files (libmf6's initialize).\n\nThe keywords are those of run_simulation: backend, precision, outdir, closures, write_output, preconditioner and precond_options.\n\nBefore the first prepare_time_step (or ModflowGPU.update) the heads are the starting heads and no period data is applied. End with finalize_simulation, which closes the output files and frees the device memory.\n\nThrows an ModflowGPU.MF6InputError or MF6UnsupportedError for invalid or unsupported input (also for a simulation with more than one model).\n\nExample\n\nsim = initialize(\"path/to/simulation\"; backend = select_backend(\"auto\"))\nwhile get_current_time(sim) < get_end_time(sim)\n    ModflowGPU.update(sim) || error(\"time step did not converge\")\nend\nfinalize_simulation(sim)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.initialize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.prepare_time_step","page":"Stepping API","text":"prepare_time_step(sim::Simulation, dt = 0.0) -> sim\n\nAdvance to the next TDIS time step and, at the first step of a stress period, apply the period data (libmf6's prepare_time_step). dt is ignored, as in libmf6 (no adaptive time stepping). Values set through the stepping API before this call are kept unless the period has new data for that package (as in MF6). Values written into host mirrors (get_value_ptr) are sent to the device first.\n\nAllowed after initialize and after finalize_time_step; throws an ArgumentError otherwise, or when the simulation has ended.\n\nExample\n\nOne time step, call by call (what ModflowGPU.update does):\n\nprepare_time_step(sim)\nprepare_solve(sim)\nfor _ in 1:only(get_value(sim, \"SLN_1/MXITER\"))\n    ModflowGPU.solve(sim) && break\nend\nfinalize_solve(sim)\nfinalize_time_step(sim)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.prepare_time_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.prepare_solve","page":"Stepping API","text":"prepare_solve(sim::Simulation, isol = 1) -> sim\n\nStart the outer iterations of the current time step (libmf6's prepare_solve): the storage term for the step length, the heads of the previous step and the constant heads; it resets the iteration counter. Values written into host mirrors since the last call are sent to the device first, and BOUND arrays opened with ModflowGPU.device_bound! are checked. isol is the solution number; only 1 exists.\n\nAllowed after prepare_time_step; see there for an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.prepare_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.solve","page":"Stepping API","text":"ModflowGPU.solve(sim::Simulation, isol = 1) -> Bool\n\nOne outer (Picard) iteration of the current time step (libmf6's solve); returns whether the step has converged. Call it up to MXITER times (get_value(sim, \"SLN_1/MXITER\")). isol is the solution number; only 1 exists.\n\nNot exported, because solve is also exported by CommonSolve (the SciML packages) and would be ambiguous next to it: write ModflowGPU.solve. See prepare_time_step for an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.finalize_solve","page":"Stepping API","text":"finalize_solve(sim::Simulation, isol = 1) -> Bool\n\nEnd the outer iterations of the current time step (libmf6's finalize_solve): compute the flows (the SIMVALS of the stress packages) and the budget of the step. Returns whether the step converged (libmf6 reports a failure status when it did not). isol is the solution number; only 1 exists. See prepare_time_step for an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.finalize_solve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.finalize_time_step","page":"Stepping API","text":"finalize_time_step(sim::Simulation) -> Bool\n\nEnd the current time step (libmf6's finalize_time_step): write the output selected by OC (head file, budget file, listing file). Returns false when the step did not converge and the simulation has no CONTINUE option (libmf6 returns a failure status; run_simulation throws a ConvergenceError there), true otherwise. See prepare_time_step for an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.finalize_time_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.do_time_step","page":"Stepping API","text":"do_time_step(sim::Simulation) -> Bool\n\nSolve the current time step: prepare_solve, up to MXITER ModflowGPU.solve calls, finalize_solve (libmf6's do_time_step). Returns whether the step converged. Call prepare_time_step before it and finalize_time_step after it.\n\nExample\n\nprepare_time_step(sim)\n# ... set boundary values of this step with set_value ...\ndo_time_step(sim) || @warn \"no convergence\"\nfinalize_time_step(sim)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.do_time_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.update","page":"Stepping API","text":"ModflowGPU.update(sim::Simulation) -> Bool\n\nRun one full time step: prepare_time_step, do_time_step, finalize_time_step (libmf6's update). Returns the result of finalize_time_step: false when the step did not converge and the simulation has no CONTINUE option.\n\nNot exported, because CUDA.jl exports an update (for CUDA graphs) and using CUDA, ModflowGPU would make update ambiguous: write ModflowGPU.update.\n\nThis is not BasicModelInterface.update, which throws a ConvergenceError in that case and returns nothing.\n\nExample\n\nsim = initialize(\"path/to/simulation\")\nModflowGPU.update(sim) # the first time step\nget_current_time(sim)  # the time at its end\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.update"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.finalize_simulation","page":"Stepping API","text":"finalize_simulation(sim::Simulation; free = true) -> sim\n\nEnd a simulation started with initialize (libmf6's finalize): close the output files and, with free (the default), release the device memory of the model now (ModflowGPU.free_device_arrays!) rather than when the garbage collector runs. This matters when another model on the same GPU allocates next.\n\nAfterwards the simulation cannot be stepped, and with free = true not read either (get_value and get_value_ptr throw); device arrays obtained before (get_value_ptr(...; device = true)) must not be used, while host mirrors keep their last values. With free = false the model stays readable (run_simulation returns it) and its memory is released by the garbage collector. A second call does nothing.\n\nModflowGPU.finalize is the same function under its earlier name.\n\nExample\n\nsim = initialize(\"path/to/simulation\")\nModflowGPU.update(sim)\nfinalize_simulation(sim)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.finalize_simulation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.finalize","page":"Stepping API","text":"ModflowGPU.finalize(sim::Simulation; free = true) -> sim\n\nThe same function as finalize_simulation, under the name used before it was exported. Not exported, because it would clash with Base.finalize; it is kept (not deprecated) for code that calls ModflowGPU.finalize.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.finalize"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.get_value","page":"Stepping API","text":"get_value(sim::Simulation, address) -> Array\nget_value(sim::Simulation, address, dest) -> dest\n\nA new host array (or dest, filled) with the current value of the variable at address (see var_addresses), read from the engine now. Values are as in get_value_ptr: Float64 with absolute heads, Int32 indices; scalars are one-element vectors.\n\nExample\n\nh = get_value(sim, \"GWF/X\")                     # heads per model node\nq = get_value(sim, \"GWF/RIV-1/SIMVALS\")         # river flows of the last step\nkper = only(get_value(sim, \"TDIS/KPER\"))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.get_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.get_value_ptr","page":"Stepping API","text":"get_value_ptr(sim::Simulation, address; device = false) -> Array\n\nA host array that mirrors the variable at address (see var_addresses), as libmf6's get_value_ptr. Values are Float64 with absolute heads (in the model's length units), indices Int32; BOUND is (ncol, maxbound), with the values before the MULTIPLIER auxiliary variable is applied.\n\nThe same array is returned on every call and stays valid until finalize_simulation. It is a copy of the engine data, refreshed at the end of initialize, prepare_time_step, prepare_solve and finalize_solve. Writes into the mirror of X or BOUND are sent to the device at the next prepare_time_step or prepare_solve; writes between prepare_solve and finalize_solve are discarded with a warning (use set_value there).\n\nWith device = true: the engine array itself, on the device (values in the precision T of the simulation, indices Int32), with head-like values as offsets from \"<model>/HREF\" and BOUND with the multiplier applied; only for X, XOLD, HREF, IBOUND, BOUND, NODELIST and SIMVALS (HREF is the host Float64 vector of the references). No mirror is made. Write a device BOUND only through ModflowGPU.device_bound!.\n\nExample\n\nheads = get_value_ptr(sim, \"GWF/X\")          # updated by every time step\nriv = get_value_ptr(sim, \"GWF/RIV-1/BOUND\")  # stage, cond, rbot per river cell\nprepare_time_step(sim)\nriv[1, :] .+= 0.1                            # raise the stages for this step\ndo_time_step(sim)\nfinalize_time_step(sim)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.get_value_ptr"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.set_value","page":"Stepping API","text":"set_value(sim::Simulation, address, values) -> sim\n\nSet the variable at address (one of input_addresses) and send it to the device now. X takes absolute heads per model node (cells with IDOMAIN > 0); BOUND takes (ncol, maxbound) values (or the same values flattened), before the MULTIPLIER auxiliary variable, with head-like columns (stage, head, elevation) absolute, in the model's units. A host mirror of the address (get_value_ptr) is updated as well. The values are used from the next solve until new period data for the package replaces them.\n\nExample\n\nprepare_time_step(sim)\nbound = get_value(sim, \"GWF/WEL-1/BOUND\")\nbound[1, :] .= -100.0                   # pumping rate of every well\nset_value(sim, \"GWF/WEL-1/BOUND\", bound)\ndo_time_step(sim)\nfinalize_time_step(sim)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.set_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.device_bound!","page":"Stepping API","text":"ModflowGPU.device_bound!(sim, address) -> device BOUND array\n\nFor coupling on the device: open the device BOUND array at address (\"<model>/<package>/BOUND\") for writing in the current time step, so that kernels of the coupled model can write boundary values without a copy to the host. Allowed only between prepare_time_step and prepare_solve (otherwise an ArgumentError). Returns the engine array, as get_value_ptr(sim, address; device = true): precision T, (ncol, maxbound), head-like columns as offsets from the reference of the record's cell (rrec), multiplier applied. prepare_solve then checks the first NBOUND records of every opened BOUND on the device (one kernel and one host-mapped flag per package): all values finite, and for RIV stage ≥ bottom and conductance ≥ 0 (riv_ck); a violation throws an MF6InputError naming the first bad record. The written values are used by the step, are replaced by new period data, and are not seen by host reads of BOUND (get_value, mirrors), which keep the values last set on the host. Writes into a device BOUND that was not opened this way are not checked (the stepping API cannot see them).\n\nNot exported: it is for code that writes device arrays in its own kernels.\n\nExample\n\nprepare_time_step(sim)\nbound = ModflowGPU.device_bound!(sim, \"GWF/RIV-1/BOUND\")   # (3, maxbound) on the device\nmy_stage_kernel!(bound, ...)   # write the stages (row 1) as offsets from the references\ndo_time_step(sim)\nfinalize_time_step(sim)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.device_bound!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.get_var_address","page":"Stepping API","text":"get_var_address(sim::Simulation, var, component; subcomponent_name = \"\") -> String\n\nThe address of variable var of component (a model name, \"SLN_1\" or \"TDIS\") and an optional subcomponent (a package name), as libmf6's get_var_address: the names in upper case, joined by /. The address is not checked; var_addresses lists the valid ones.\n\nExample\n\nget_var_address(sim, \"bound\", \"gwf\"; subcomponent_name = \"riv-1\")   # \"GWF/RIV-1/BOUND\"\nget_var_address(sim, \"x\", \"gwf\")                                     # \"GWF/X\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.get_var_address"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.var_addresses","page":"Stepping API","text":"var_addresses(sim::Simulation) -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery address that get_value, get_value_ptr and the BMI accept, as libmf6 names them, for a model <model> with stress packages <package>:\n\n<model>/X, XOLD (heads now and at the end of the previous step), HREF (the per-cell reference heads), IBOUND;\n<model>/DIS/NODEUSER, NODEREDUCED, TOP, BOT, AREA, NODES, NODESUSER;\n<model>/<package>/BOUND, NODELIST, SIMVALS (simulated flows), NBOUND, MAXBOUND;\nSLN_1/MXITER and TDIS/DELT, TOTIM, PERTIM, KPER, KSTP, NPER.\n\nThe writable ones are listed by input_addresses.\n\nExample\n\nsim = initialize(\"path/to/simulation\")\nfilter(contains(\"/RIV\"), var_addresses(sim))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.var_addresses"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.input_addresses","page":"Stepping API","text":"input_addresses(sim::Simulation) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe addresses that set_value accepts (and whose get_value_ptr mirrors may be written): \"<model>/X\" and \"<model>/<package>/BOUND\" of every stress package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.input_addresses"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.get_current_time","page":"Stepping API","text":"get_current_time(sim::Simulation) -> Float64\n\nThe simulation time at the end of the current time step (TDIS totim, in the model's time units; 0 before the first prepare_time_step).\n\nExample\n\nsim = initialize(\"path/to/simulation\")\nget_current_time(sim)   # 0.0\nModflowGPU.update(sim)\nget_current_time(sim)   # the length of the first time step\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.get_current_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.get_start_time","page":"Stepping API","text":"get_start_time(sim::Simulation) -> Float64\n\nThe start time of the simulation: always 0.0, in the model's time units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.get_start_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.get_end_time","page":"Stepping API","text":"get_end_time(sim::Simulation) -> Float64\n\nThe end time of the simulation: the sum of the stress-period lengths (TDIS PERLEN), in the model's time units.\n\nExample\n\nwhile get_current_time(sim) < get_end_time(sim)\n    ModflowGPU.update(sim)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.get_end_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/stepping/#ModflowGPU.get_time_step","page":"Stepping API","text":"get_time_step(sim::Simulation) -> Float64\n\nThe length of the current time step (TDIS delt, in the model's time units, as libmf6's get_time_step; before the first step, that of the first step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.get_time_step"},{"category":"section","location":"compatibility/results/#Comparing-results","page":"Comparing results","text":"This page is not written yet. It will cover how close the results are to MODFLOW 6 and how that is checked.\n\nUntil then, the developer notes describe the current behaviour.","title":"Comparing results"},{"category":"section","location":"coupling/bmi/#BMI","page":"BMI","text":"This page is not written yet. It will cover the Basic Model Interface methods and what they mean for each grid type.\n\nUntil then, the developer notes describe the current behaviour.","title":"BMI"},{"category":"section","location":"developers/#For-developers","page":"For developers","text":"This section is for people who change ModflowGPU. The developer material is not part of the manual; it lives in the repository.\n\nPLAN.md: goals, architecture, conventions and the roadmap.\nCLAUDE.md: working rules for agents and contributors (git workflow, precision rules, tests).\ndocs/dev/: developer notes. Start with api_map.md (map of src/), support_matrix.md (what is supported, card by card), mf6_notes.md (how MODFLOW 6 behaviour is mapped), precision.md and scaling.md.\ndocs/dev/plan/: the task cards, one file per phase.\nbenchmark/results/: committed benchmark results.","title":"For developers"},{"category":"section","location":"developers/#Running-the-tests","page":"For developers","text":"julia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'                    # CPU tests (what CI runs)\nMFGPU_TEST_BACKEND=rocm julia --project=test test/runtests.jl # GPU tests, on a machine with a GPU\nMFGPU_TEST_GROUPS=connectivity,npf julia --project=test test/runtests.jl # selected groups\njulia --project=@runic -m Runic --check .                     # formatting","title":"Running the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"developers/#Building-this-manual","page":"For developers","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe site is written to docs/build/. Warnings fail the build. A change that alters what users see (a new package or option, a changed keyword, a new address) should update the matching page under docs/src/ in the same change.","title":"Building this manual"},{"category":"section","location":"#ModflowGPU.jl","page":"Home","text":"ModflowGPU.jl runs the groundwater-flow model (GWF) of MODFLOW 6 on a GPU or a CPU. It reads ordinary MODFLOW 6 input files (mfsim.nam and the files it lists), writes the usual head, budget and listing files, and can also be driven one time step at a time from Julia, for example to couple it to a land-surface or hydrology model. It is written in Julia with KernelAbstractions.jl, so the same code can run on AMD, NVIDIA, Intel and Apple GPUs, on OpenCL devices and on the CPU. Only AMD (ROCm) and the CPU are tested so far.\n\nMODFLOW 6 is the reference. A feature is only accepted when it reproduces MODFLOW 6 heads and volumetric budgets on the same input. The first user is ModflowCoupling, which couples MODFLOW 6 to the mGV hydrology model for basin-to-global groundwater models.","title":"ModflowGPU.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Status","page":"Home","text":"The package is under development and not yet registered; install it from its Git URL (see Installation). Supported today:\n\none GWF model with one IMS solution, on DIS, DISV and DISU grids;\nNPF (confined and convertible cells, wetting and drying, anisotropy), STO, IC and OC;\nthe boundary packages CHD, WEL, GHB, RIV, DRN, RCH and EVT, with time series (TS) and time-array series (TAS);\nthe Picard outer iteration with CG or BiCGSTAB inner solvers, in double, mixed or single precision, with volumetric budgets;\nthe stepping API and BMI for coupling.\n\nNot supported yet: Newton-Raphson, XT3D, several models with exchanges, and observation (OBS) output. Input that uses something unsupported is rejected with an error that names it, never silently ignored. Packages for other processes (SFR, LAK, MAW, UZF, MVR, CSUB and the transport models) are out of scope. See Supported packages.","title":"Status"},{"category":"section","location":"#When-to-use-it","page":"Home","text":"You have a large MODFLOW 6 GWF model (millions of cells, for example a regional or global model) and a GPU, and want shorter run times.\nYou run MODFLOW 6 inside another model, one time step at a time, and the groundwater part has become the slow part.\nYou want to write your own coupling in Julia and need direct access to heads, boundary data and budgets as arrays.","title":"When to use it"},{"category":"section","location":"#When-not-to-use-it","page":"Home","text":"Your model needs a package or option listed as unsupported. Use MODFLOW 6.\nYour model is small. A GPU only pays off when there is enough work to keep it busy, so on a model of a few thousand cells do not expect a speed-up over MODFLOW 6 on the CPU.\nYou need identical iteration counts or listing files. ModflowGPU uses a different preconditioner, so iteration counts differ; heads and budgets agree within the closure criteria of the model.\nYou need MODFLOW 6's transport models or its full set of output files.","title":"When not to use it"},{"category":"section","location":"#How-to-cite","page":"Home","text":"There is no paper yet. Until a release is tagged, cite the repository:\n\nSchilperoort, B. ModflowGPU.jl: MODFLOW 6 groundwater flow on GPUs in Julia. https://github.com/BSchilperoort/ModflowGPU.jl\n\nPlease also cite MODFLOW 6 (Langevin et al., 2017, doi:10.3133/tm6A55), whose equations and input format this package implements.","title":"How to cite"},{"category":"section","location":"#AI-declaration","page":"Home","text":"This package is developed with AI coding agents. The plan was written with AI assistance and is carried out one task at a time: an agent writes the code, tests and documentation on a branch, and a separate agent reviews it. The owner sets the scope and makes design decisions. Correctness is checked against MODFLOW 6 itself, not by the agents' own judgement. All of the code was written by AI. The full declaration, in the AI Declaration Format, is in aidecl.yaml.","title":"AI declaration"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Installation and Getting started for a first run.\nSupported packages to check that your model is supported.\nStepping API to drive the model from Julia.\nFor developers if you want to change the code.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Installation","page":"Installation","text":"ModflowGPU is a Julia package. You need Julia, the package itself and, to run on a GPU, the Julia package for your GPU. Without a GPU package everything runs on the CPU, which is enough to try the package and to follow Getting started.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Julia","page":"Installation","text":"ModflowGPU needs Julia 1.12 or newer. The easiest way to install and update Julia is juliaup, the official installer; the Julia download page has the one-line install command for your operating system. Check the version in a terminal:\n\njulia --version   # julia version 1.12.x or newer\n\nIf it shows an older version, run juliaup update (or juliaup add release followed by juliaup default release).\n\nModflowGPU is developed and tested on Linux. It contains no Linux-specific code, but it has not been tested on Windows or macOS.","title":"Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#The-package","page":"Installation","text":"ModflowGPU is not yet in Julia's General registry, so you add it by its Git URL. Use a project environment of your own, so that the packages of different projects do not get in each other's way. In a terminal, in the folder where you keep your work:\n\njulia --project=.\n\nand then in Julia:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/BSchilperoort/ModflowGPU.jl\")\n\nThis downloads ModflowGPU and its dependencies and writes Project.toml and Manifest.toml in the folder. The first time takes one to a few minutes, because Julia also precompiles the packages. Start Julia with julia --project=. in that folder from then on.\n\nTo get a newer version of ModflowGPU later, run Pkg.update(\"ModflowGPU\").\n\nCheck that it works:\n\njulia> using ModflowGPU\n\njulia> backend = select_backend(\"cpu\");\n\njulia> backend_name(backend)\n\"POCLBackend\"\n\nThe CPU backend is POCL, a portable OpenCL implementation that is installed with ModflowGPU's dependencies; you do not need to install anything else for it. It uses as many threads as Julia was started with: start Julia with julia --project=. --threads=auto to use all cores.","title":"The package"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#A-GPU","page":"Installation","text":"Each GPU vendor has its own Julia package. ModflowGPU supports them as optional extensions: you add the GPU package to the same environment and load it next to ModflowGPU, and ModflowGPU then finds the GPU. Pick the section for your GPU below.\n\nwarning: Only AMD GPUs are tested\nModflowGPU is developed and tested on an AMD GPU (Radeon RX 7800 XT, ROCm 7.2) and on the CPU. For NVIDIA (CUDA), Intel (oneAPI), Apple (Metal) and other OpenCL devices, the extension is written and loads together with the other GPU packages, but no model has been run on such a GPU yet. Please report what you find in an issue.\n\nGPU Julia package Backend name Float64 Tested on hardware\nAMD (Radeon, Instinct) AMDGPU (2.11 or newer) \"rocm\" yes yes\nNVIDIA CUDA (6.5 or newer) \"cuda\" yes no\nIntel (Arc, Data Center GPU) oneAPI (2.10 or newer) \"oneapi\" depends on the GPU no\nApple (M-series) Metal (1.12 or newer) \"metal\" no no\nOther OpenCL devices OpenCL (1 or newer) \"opencl\" depends on the device no\n\nThe Float64 column matters for the precision presets: on a GPU without Float64, ModflowGPU runs in single precision (:single), which is less accurate.","title":"A GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#AMD:-AMDGPU.jl","page":"Installation","text":"AMDGPU.jl uses the ROCm libraries installed on your system. Install ROCm first, following AMD's ROCm installation guide for Linux, and check that rocminfo lists your GPU. ROCm is normally installed in /opt/rocm, where AMDGPU.jl finds it. Then add the package:\n\nPkg.add(\"AMDGPU\")\n\nCheck that Julia sees the GPU (needs an AMD GPU):\n\nusing AMDGPU\nAMDGPU.functional()    # true\nAMDGPU.versioninfo()   # ROCm version and the GPUs found\n\nFloat64 is available on AMD GPUs.","title":"AMD: AMDGPU.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#NVIDIA:-CUDA.jl","page":"Installation","text":"CUDA.jl needs only the NVIDIA driver; it downloads the matching CUDA toolkit itself. Check that nvidia-smi lists your GPU, then add the package:\n\nPkg.add(\"CUDA\")\n\nCheck (needs an NVIDIA GPU):\n\nusing CUDA\nCUDA.functional()    # true\nCUDA.versioninfo()   # driver, toolkit and the GPUs found\n\nFloat64 is available on NVIDIA GPUs. On many consumer cards it is much slower than Float32; this has not been measured with ModflowGPU.","title":"NVIDIA: CUDA.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Intel:-oneAPI.jl","page":"Installation","text":"oneAPI.jl needs the Intel GPU driver with Level Zero support (Linux). Add the package:\n\nPkg.add(\"oneAPI\")\n\nCheck (needs an Intel GPU):\n\nusing oneAPI\noneAPI.functional()    # true\noneAPI.versioninfo()\n\nSome Intel GPUs, for example the Arc series, have no Float64. ModflowGPU asks the device and then runs in single precision (see supports_float64).","title":"Intel: oneAPI.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Apple:-Metal.jl","page":"Installation","text":"Metal.jl works on Macs with Apple silicon (M-series). Add the package:\n\nPkg.add(\"Metal\")\n\nCheck (needs an Apple GPU):\n\nusing Metal\nMetal.functional()    # true\nMetal.versioninfo()\n\nApple GPUs have no Float64, so ModflowGPU always runs in single precision on them.","title":"Apple: Metal.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Other-OpenCL-devices:-OpenCL.jl","page":"Installation","text":"OpenCL.jl runs on any device with an OpenCL driver that can run SPIR-V kernels. Install the driver of your device, then add the package:\n\nPkg.add(\"OpenCL\")\n\nFloat64 depends on the device (the cl_khr_fp64 extension); ModflowGPU asks the device.","title":"Other OpenCL devices: OpenCL.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Check-that-the-GPU-is-found","page":"Installation","text":"Load the GPU package before or together with ModflowGPU, then ask ModflowGPU which backend it picks:\n\nusing AMDGPU, ModflowGPU         # or CUDA, oneAPI, Metal, OpenCL; needs a GPU\n\nbackend = select_backend(\"auto\") # the first GPU that works, else the CPU\nbackend_name(backend)            # \"ROCBackend\" on an AMD GPU, \"POCLBackend\" for the CPU\nsupports_float64(backend)        # true on AMD and NVIDIA GPUs\nresolve_precision(backend)       # the precision `:auto` gives on this backend\n\nbackend_options lists every name select_backend accepts, also for GPU packages that are not loaded:\n\njulia> backend_options()\n7-element Vector{String}:\n \"auto\"\n \"cpu\"\n \"cuda\"\n \"rocm\"\n \"metal\"\n \"oneapi\"\n \"opencl\"\n\nselect_backend(\"auto\") never fails: without a working GPU it returns the CPU backend. Ask for a GPU by name to get an error that says what is missing instead:\n\njulia> select_backend(\"rocm\")\nERROR: Backend 'rocm' requires AMDGPU.jl. Add it to your environment and load it alongside ModflowGPU, e.g. `using AMDGPU, ModflowGPU`.\n[...]\n\nIf the package is loaded but cannot use the GPU (no driver, no device), the error says AMDGPU is not functional on this machine; check the package's own functional() and versioninfo() as above.","title":"Check that the GPU is found"},{"category":"section","location":"install/#Next-steps","page":"Installation","text":"Getting started: run a first model and read its results.\nGPU guide: precision, memory use and troubleshooting.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/output/#Output-files","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU writes the same output files as MF6: the head file (.hds), the budget file (.cbc), the binary grid file (.dis.grb and the like) and the model listing file (.lst). The readers on this page read these files from ModflowGPU and from MF6 alike, so they can also be used to compare the two. The binary files can be read by FloPy as well.\n\nHead and budget files are indexed when opened (the record headers only); the data of a record is read when asked for.\n\nusing ModflowGPU\nhf = open_head_file(\"path/to/simulation/model.hds\")\nh = read_head(hf, length(hf))            # the last saved heads, (ncol, nrow, nlay)\n\nbf = open_budget_file(\"path/to/simulation/model.cbc\")\nwel = read_budget(bf; text = \"WEL\")      # one BudgetList per time step","title":"Output files"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.open_head_file","page":"Output files","text":"open_head_file(path; grid = :auto) -> HeadFile\n\nIndex the head file at path: read every record header (not the data) and group the layer records of each saved array. grid sets the shape of the arrays returned by read_head: :dis gives (ncol, nrow, nlay), :disv (ncpl, nlay), :disu a vector of nodes, and :auto the record layout (m1, m2, nlay) (which is the DIS shape). All values are Float64, in MF6's user-node order.\n\nReads head files of ModflowGPU and of MF6 alike. Throws an ModflowGPU.MF6BinaryFileError for a malformed file.\n\nExample\n\nhf = open_head_file(\"path/to/simulation/model.hds\")\nhead_times(hf)                   # the times of the saved arrays\nh = read_head(hf, length(hf))    # the last one, (ncol, nrow, nlay) for DIS\nh = read_head(hf; kper = 2, kstp = 1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.open_head_file"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.HeadFile","page":"Output files","text":"HeadFile\n\nIndex of an MF6 head file, built by open_head_file: the path, the grid layout used to shape arrays (:dis, :disv, :disu or :auto) and the saved arrays (steps, one ModflowGPU.HeadStep each, in file order). Data are read lazily by read_head. length(hf) is the number of saved arrays.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.HeadFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.HeadStep","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU.HeadStep\n\nOne saved array of a head file (all its layer records), an element of HeadFile.steps: kstp, kper (time step and stress period), pertim, totim (time in the period and in the simulation), text (16 characters as in the file, e.g. \"HEAD            \"), m1, m2 (per-layer record size), nlay (number of layer records) and the byte offsets of the data of each layer record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.HeadStep"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.read_head","page":"Output files","text":"read_head(hf, i) -> Array{Float64}\nread_head(hf; kstp, kper, totim, text) -> Array{Float64}\n\nRead the i-th saved array of the head file (or the one selected by find_head_step), shaped as set by the grid of open_head_file. Heads are in the model's length units; cells outside the model hold ModflowGPU.HNOFLO, dry cells ModflowGPU.HDRY. See open_head_file for an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.read_head"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.find_head_step","page":"Output files","text":"find_head_step(hf; kstp = nothing, kper = nothing, totim = nothing, text = nothing) -> Int\n\nIndex of the saved array matching all given keys (text is compared without the padding blanks and case-insensitively). Throws an ArgumentError if there is no match or more than one.\n\nExample\n\ni = find_head_step(hf; kper = 1, kstp = 10)\nread_head(hf, i)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.find_head_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.head_kstpkper","page":"Output files","text":"head_kstpkper(hf) -> Vector{Tuple{Int, Int}}\n\n(kstp, kper) of each saved array of the head file, in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.head_kstpkper"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.head_times","page":"Output files","text":"head_times(hf) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nSimulation time (totim) of each saved array of the head file, in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.head_times"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.HNOFLO","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU.HNOFLO = 1.0e30\n\nThe head written to head files for cells outside the model (IDOMAIN ≤ 0), as MF6 (DHNOFLO in Constants.f90).\n\nExample\n\nh = read_head(open_head_file(\"model.hds\"), 1)\nh[h .== ModflowGPU.HNOFLO] .= NaN\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.HNOFLO"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.HDRY","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU.HDRY = -1.0e30\n\nThe head written to head files for dry cells, as MF6 (DHDRY in Constants.f90).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.HDRY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.open_budget_file","page":"Output files","text":"open_budget_file(path) -> BudgetFile\n\nIndex the budget file (.cbc) at path: read the header of every record (not the data). Only compact records with IMETH 1 or 6, the ones MF6 writes, are supported. Reads budget files of ModflowGPU and of MF6 alike. Throws an ModflowGPU.MF6BinaryFileError for a malformed file.\n\nExample\n\nbf = open_budget_file(\"path/to/simulation/model.cbc\")\nbudget_texts(bf)        # e.g. [\"FLOW-JA-FACE\", \"DATA-SPDIS\", \"WEL\", \"RIV\", ...]\nbudget_kstpkper(bf)     # [(1, 1), (1, 2), ...]\nriv = read_budget(bf; text = \"RIV\", kper = 2, kstp = 1)   # one BudgetList\nsum(only(riv).q)        # net river flow into the model (L³/T)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.open_budget_file"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.BudgetFile","page":"Output files","text":"BudgetFile\n\nIndex of a budget file, built by open_budget_file: the path and the header of every record (records, ModflowGPU.BudgetRecords in file order). Data are read lazily by read_budget. length(bf) is the number of records.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.BudgetFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.BudgetRecord","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU.BudgetRecord\n\nHeader of one record of a budget file, an element of BudgetFile.records, as indexed by open_budget_file:\n\nkstp, kper, text (16 characters as in the file, e.g. \"    FLOW-JA-FACE\"), ndim1, ndim2, ndim3 (the grid dimensions; ndim3 as written, i.e. negative for MF6's compact header), imeth, delt, pertim, totim;\nfor IMETH 6: modelnam1, paknam1, modelnam2, paknam2 (16 characters each), auxtxt (auxiliary names, 16 characters each); empty strings for IMETH 1;\nn: number of values (IMETH 1) or list entries (IMETH 6), and offset, the byte offset of the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.BudgetRecord"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.BudgetList","page":"Output files","text":"BudgetList\n\nThe entries of an IMETH 6 budget record, as returned by read_budget: id1, id2 (Int32 node numbers; for a stress package id1 is the model cell (user node) and id2 the package entry), q (flow, L³/T, positive into the model cell id1), and aux (naux × nlist matrix of auxiliary values, in the order of the record's auxtxt). length(list) is the number of entries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.BudgetList"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.read_budget","page":"Output files","text":"read_budget(bf, i) -> Vector{Float64} or BudgetList\nread_budget(bf; text, kstp, kper, ...) -> Vector\n\nRead the data of record i: a vector of n values for IMETH 1 (user-node order, reshape(v, ndim1, ndim2, -ndim3) gives the grid shape; FLOW-JA-FACE is a vector of length NJA), a BudgetList for IMETH 6. The keyword form reads all records matching find_budget_records, in file order. Flows are in L³/T in the model's units. See open_budget_file for an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.read_budget"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.find_budget_records","page":"Output files","text":"find_budget_records(bf; text, kstp, kper, totim, imeth, paknam2, modelnam2) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices of the records matching all given keys. Texts and names are compared without padding and case-insensitively. Note that GWF-GWF exchange flows are IMETH 6 records with text FLOW-JA-FACE (and the exchange name as paknam2); use imeth = 1 for the model's own FLOW-JA-FACE array.\n\nExample\n\nidx = find_budget_records(bf; text = \"STO-SS\")\n[bf.records[i].totim for i in idx]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.find_budget_records"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.budget_texts","page":"Output files","text":"budget_texts(bf) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe distinct record texts of the budget file bf (BudgetFile), without padding, in order of first appearance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.budget_texts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.budget_kstpkper","page":"Output files","text":"budget_kstpkper(bf) -> Vector{Tuple{Int, Int}}\n\nThe distinct (kstp, kper) (time step, stress period) of the budget file bf, in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.budget_kstpkper"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.budget_times","page":"Output files","text":"budget_times(bf) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe distinct simulation times (totim, in the model's time units) of the budget file bf, in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.budget_times"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.read_grb","page":"Output files","text":"read_grb(path) -> BinaryGridFile\n\nRead an MF6 binary grid file (.dis.grb, .disv.grb, .disu.grb), written by ModflowGPU or MF6 for the DIS, DISV or DISU package. It holds the grid and the model's connectivity (IA, JA), which give the meaning of the FLOW-JA-FACE budget record. Throws an ModflowGPU.MF6BinaryFileError for a malformed file.\n\nExample\n\ng = read_grb(\"path/to/simulation/model.dis.grb\")\ng.grid                  # \"DIS\"\ng[\"NCOL\"], g[\"NROW\"], g[\"NLAY\"]\nia, ja = g[\"IA\"], g[\"JA\"]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.read_grb"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.BinaryGridFile","page":"Output files","text":"BinaryGridFile\n\nContents of an MF6 binary grid file (see read_grb): grid (\"DIS\", \"DISV\" or \"DISU\"), version, the four raw header lines, lentxt, the variables in file order and their data by name (INTEGER as Int32, DOUBLE as Float64; scalars as numbers, NDIM 1 variables as vectors, NDIM 2 as matrices with the dimensions in the file's (Fortran) order). g[name] and haskey(g, name) access data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.BinaryGridFile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.GrbVariable","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU.GrbVariable\n\nOne variable of a binary grid file, an element of BinaryGridFile.variables: name (e.g. \"IA\"), type (:INTEGER or :DOUBLE), dims (empty for a scalar) and definition, the definition line as written in the file (LENTXT characters, including the trailing newline).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.GrbVariable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.read_listing_budgets","page":"Output files","text":"read_listing_budgets(path) -> Vector{ListingBudget}\n\nAll budget tables (... BUDGET FOR ... AT END OF TIME STEP ...) of a model listing file (.lst) of ModflowGPU or MF6, in file order, with the values as printed (rates L³/T, volumes L³). Throws an ModflowGPU.MF6InputError for a table it cannot read.\n\nExample\n\ntables = read_listing_budgets(\"path/to/simulation/model.lst\")\nlast(tables).rate_percent_discrepancy\n[(t.name, t.rate_in, t.rate_out) for t in last(tables).terms]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.read_listing_budgets"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.ListingBudget","page":"Output files","text":"ListingBudget\n\nOne budget table of a listing file: title (e.g. \"VOLUME\"), zone (e.g. \"ENTIRE MODEL\"), kstp, kper (0 if the printed number overflowed its field), the terms (ModflowGPU.ListingBudgetTerm), the totals total_cum_in, total_cum_out, total_rate_in, total_rate_out, the differences cum_in_minus_out, rate_in_minus_out and the printed cum_percent_discrepancy, rate_percent_discrepancy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.ListingBudget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.ListingBudgetTerm","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU.ListingBudgetTerm\n\nOne term of a listing-file budget table, an element of ListingBudget.terms: name (budget text, e.g. \"WEL\", \"STO-SS\"), package (package name column, \"\" when the table is unlabeled), cumulative volumes cum_in, cum_out and rates rate_in, rate_out (both non-negative, as printed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.ListingBudgetTerm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.listing_rates","page":"Output files","text":"listing_rates(path, term; title = \"VOLUME\", zone = \"ENTIRE MODEL\")\n    -> Vector{Tuple{Float64, Float64}}\n\n(rate in, rate out) of the budget term term in every budget table of the listing file with the given title and zone (by default the model's volume budget; e.g. one table per stress period with PRINT BUDGET LAST), summed over the rows whose name or package name equals term (case-insensitive). Ported from the listing_rates helper of ModflowCoupling's tests, which took the first matching row; summing gives the same for a single package and the model total for several packages of one type. Rates are in L³/T in the model's units.\n\nExample\n\nlisting_rates(\"path/to/simulation/model.lst\", \"RIV\")   # [(in, out) per table]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.listing_rates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.percent_discrepancy","page":"Output files","text":"percent_discrepancy(total_in, total_out) -> Float64\npercent_discrepancy(b::ModelBudget) -> Float64\n\nMF6's percent discrepancy 100 (in - out) / ((in + out) / 2), zero when both are zero (budget_ot in Budget.f90). For a ModelBudget: of its total rates in and out (see cum_percent_discrepancy for the cumulative volumes).\n\nExample\n\njulia> percent_discrepancy(101.0, 99.0)\n2.0\n\njulia> percent_discrepancy(0.0, 0.0)\n0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.percent_discrepancy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/output/#ModflowGPU.MF6BinaryFileError","page":"Output files","text":"ModflowGPU.MF6BinaryFileError(path, offset, msg)\n\nError raised by the output readers (open_head_file, open_budget_file, read_grb) when an MF6 binary output file is malformed or truncated: offset is the byte offset in the file where the problem was found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ModflowGPU.MF6BinaryFileError"}]
}
